    "ls|LS17_S11_U_250": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.250 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 3RD FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nAYUSH MEDICINES \n \n250. SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n     \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the status of functional health and wellness centres under National AYUSH Mission in all \nthe districts of Bihar during the last three years; \n(b) whether the Government has taken any initiatives to regulate and control AYUSH \nmedicines in Supaul district; and \n(c)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) Ministry of Ayush is implementing the operationalization of AYUSH Health and \nWellness Centres (AHWCs) under Ayushman Bharat as a component of the Centrally Sponsored \nScheme of National AYUSH Mission (NAM) throu gh State/UT Governments with an objective \nto establish a holistic wellness model based on AYUSH principles and practices.  As per the \nreports received from State Government of Bihar , 18 AYUSH Health and wellness centres are \nmade functional during the last 3 years. The district –wise status of functional AYUSH Health \nand Wellness Centres during the last three years in the State of Bihar are furnished at Annexure.  \n \n(b) & (c) As prescribed in Drugs and Cosmetics Act 1940 and Rules 1945 made thereunder, \nenforcement of the legal provisions pertaining to Quality Control and issuance of drug license of \nAyurveda, Siddha, Unani and Homoeopathy drugs, is vested with the State Drug Controllers/ \nState Licensing Authorities appointed by the concerned State/ Union Territory Government. Rule \n158-B in the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 provides the regulatory guidelines for issue of \nlicense to manufacture Ayurvedic, Siddha, Unani medicines and Rule 85 (A to I) in the Drugs \nand Cosmetics Rules, 1945 provides the regulatory guidelines for issue of license to manufacture \nHomoeopathy medicines. It is mandatory for the manufacturers to adhere to the prescribed \nrequirements for licensing of manufacturing units & medicines including proof of safety & \neffectiveness, compliance with the Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) as per Schedule T & \nSchedule M-I of Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 and quality standards of drugs given in the \nrespective pharmacopoeia. \n \n \n \n\n2 \n \nAnnexure \n \nDistrict –wise status of functional AYUSH HWCs during the last three years \n \nSl.No. Name of \nState/UT \nName of \nDistrict \nFunctional AYUSH HWCs \n1  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nBihar \n \nAurangabad \n \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Goh, Rafigang \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Deo,  \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Deokund Barun \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Pipra Baghi, \nObera  \n2 Begusarai \n \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Binodpur, \nBarauni \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Katahri, \nSahebpur Kamal \n3 Gopalganj \n \nState Ayurvedic DispensaryPashurampur   \nManjha  \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Thawe \nKochaykot \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Pakri Tola \nVijaipur \n4 Nalanda State Ayurvedic Dispensary Hargawa, \nGiriyak \n5 Nawada State Ayurvedic Dispensary Warsligang, \nAkabarpur \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Sonsarai, \nGovindpur \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Darma, Narhat \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Dona, Pakri \nBarawa \n6 Siwan \n \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Gyaspur, \nPachrukhi \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Basantpur, \nBasantpur \n7 West Champaran State Ayurvedic Dispensary Peepara, \nChanpatiya  \nState Ayurvedic Dispensary Majhauliya \nGrand Total 18 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_251": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.251 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3RD FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nSAAPS UNDER NAM \n \n251.  SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND:  \n    SHRI ANURAG SHARMA:  \n    DR. T. SUMATHY (A) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN:   \n  \n \n Will the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has considered the State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs) of \nTamil Nadu and other States under National AYUSH Mission (NAM) for the year 2022 -23 and \nif so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the funds allocated to Tamil Nadu under various schemes and programs of National AYUSH \nMission (NAM) including setting up of AYUSH Hospitals/Health and wellness centres and \nrecurring expenses under various heads; and  \n \n(c) the effective steps taken by t he Government to develop the AYUSH system of medicine and \ninfrastructure in the country including Tamil Nadu alongwith the funds allocated for the same \nduring the last five years and the current year? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTEROF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) Under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH Mission (NAM), Ministry of \nAyush has approved the submitted State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs) of different State/UT \nGovernments including  Tamil Nadu for the year 2022 -23 as per the provisions of NAM \nguidelines. The State/UT-wise detailed status of approv al of SAAPs including Tamil Nadu is \nfurnished at Annexure-I. \n \n(b) Under NAM, as per the proposals received from State Government of Tamil Nadu through \nSAAP during the year 2022-23, Ministry of Ayush has approved/allocated the funds to the State \nGovernment for different activities/programmes as per the provision of NAM guidelines   \nincluding setting  up of AYUSH Hospitals, Health and Wellness C entres and other recurring \nactivities. The activity wise status of approved/allocated funds is furnished at Annexure-II. \n \n(c)  Ministry of Ayush is implementing NAM through State/UT Governments including Tamil \nNadu and supporting the efforts of States/UTs for overall development of AYUSH systems of \n\n2 \n \nmedicine including infrastructural development by providing financial assistance to them under \ndifferent activities as per the provision of NAM guideline in accordance to the proposal received \nin their State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs). The Mission  inter-alia makes provision for the \nfollowing activities: - \n \n(i) AYUSH Health & Wellness Centres \n(ii) Co -location of AYUSH facilities at Primary Health Centres ( PHCs), Community Health \nCentres (CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs) \n(iii) Upgradation of existing standalone Government AYUSH Hospitals \n(iv)Upgradation of existing Government/ Panchayat/Government aided AYUSH \nDispensaries/Construction of building for existing AYUSH Dispensary (Rented/ dilapidated \naccommodation)/Construction of building to establish new AYUSH Dispensary in the area \nwhere there are no AYUSH facilities available \n(v) Setting up of upto 10/30/50 bedded integrated AYUSH Hospitals \n(vi) Supply of essential drugs to Government AYUSH Hospitals, Government Dispensaries and \nGovernment/Government aided Teaching Institutional AYUSH Hospitals \n(vii) AYUSH Public Health Programmes \n(viii) Behaviour Change Communication (BCC) \n(ix) Mobility support at State and District level \n(x) AYUSH Gram \n(xi) Establishment of new AYUSH colleges in the States  where availability of AYUSH teaching \ninstitutions is inadequate in Government Sector \n(xii) Infrastructural development of AYUSH Under-Graduate Institutions \n(xiii) Infrastructural development of AYUSH Post -Graduate Institutions/ add on PG/ Pharmacy \n/Para-Medical Courses \n  \n Under NAM, the State/UT-wise details of funds allocated /approved against the submitted \nSAAPs for the overall development of AYUSH systems of medicine including infrastructural \ndevelopment during the last five years and the current year is furnished at Annexure-III. \n\n3 \n \nAnnexure-I \nState/UT-wise status of approval of SAAPs under NAM \nSl. No.  Name of the State/UT Approval of SAAP \n(Central + State)   \n(Rs. in lakhs) \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar Islands 284.783 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 2186.080 \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh 1786.840 \n4.  Assam 4496.770 \n5.  Bihar 9311.916 \n6.  Chandigarh 758.938 \n7.  Chhattisgarh 4079.120 \n8.  Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 1208.770 \n9.  Goa 949.904 \n10.  Gujarat 6362.085 \n11.  Haryana 2710.900 \n12.  Himachal Pradesh 8607.956 \n13.  Jammu & Kashmir 7251.980 \n14.  Jharkhand 25841.898 \n15.  Karnataka 5713.620 \n16.  Kerala 9777.401 \n17.  Ladakh 289.060 \n18.  Lakshadweep 232.644 \n19.  Madhya Pradesh 11441.777 \n20.  Maharashtra 5084.440 \n21.  Manipur 7657.800 \n22.  Mizoram 521.200 \n23.  Meghalaya 3541.529 \n24.  Nagaland 2203.460 \n25.  Odisha 5175.642 \n26.  Puducherry 2079.875 \n27.  Punjab 1305.320 \n28.  Rajasthan 17055.073 \n29.  Sikkim 695.630 \n30.  Tamil Nadu 11701.623 \n31.  Telangana 3847.270 \n32.  Tripura 2290.896 \n33.  Uttar Pradesh 48125.322 \n34.  Uttarakhand 5499.260 \n35.  West Bengal 7043.878 \n  Total 227120.660 \n \n \n \n\n4 \n \nAnnexure-II \nActivity wise status of funds approved/allocated to Tamil Nadu during 2022-23 \nunder NAM  \n(Rs. in lakhs) \nSl. \nNo. \nActivity Recurring Non- Recurring Total \n1.  AYUSH Health & Wellness Centres 2164.60 2740.000 \n \n4904.60 \n2.  Supply Essential Drugs AYUSH \nDispensaries \n1641.00 00 1641.00 \n3.  Supply of Essential Drugs & \nConsumables \n750.00 00 750.00 \n4.  Mobility Support 50.600 00 50.600 \n5.  Supply of Medicine to School Health \nProgramme \n00 00 00 \n6.  National Program for Prevention  and \nManagement of Musculoskele tal \nDisorders \n450.000 00 450.00 \n7.  VAYO MITRA AYUSH Geriatric \nHealth Care Services \n120.000 00 120.00 \n8.  Establishment of New AYUSH \nDispensary in the area where there is no \nAYUSH facilities available. \n00 30.000 30.000 \n9.  Setting up of AYUSH wings in District \nHospitals \n00 175.00 175.00 \n10.  Setting up of  50 Integrated AYUSH \nHospital, Namakkal \n00 200.00 200.00 \n11.  Establishment of AYUSH OPD clinics in \nthe PHCs at 30 Places in Tamil Nadu  \n00 600.00 600.00 \n12.  Establishment of AYUSH Mobile \nMedical Unit at 10 District \n94.25 00 94.25 \n13.  Ongoing Activity Approved balance \nGrants for Previous year SAAP 2021-22 \n838.413 46.50 884.913 \n14.  Setting up of Smart Class Room at \nGovernment Siddha Medical College, \nChennai \n00 20.00 20.00 \n15.  Setting up of Smart Class Room with \nprojector, Screen, Laptop at Government \nYoga & Naturopathy Medical College, \nChennai \n00 15.00 15.00 \n16.  Setting up of Smart Classes  at  \nGovernment Unani Medical College, \nChennai \n00 15.00 15.00 \n17.  Establishment of new AYUSH Colleg es \nby upgrading AYUSH Hospitals at \nPalani, Dindugul District \n00 1000.00 1000.00 \n18.  Yoga Wellness Centre 204.000 00 204.000 \n19.  Hiring charges Payable to Personnel \nengaged in 38 AYUSH Wellness Centres \n228.00 00 228.00 \n\n5 \n \n20.  Two Tribal units for Supply of \nMedicines and Manpower \n34.360 00 34.360 \n21.  Manpower for Health & Wellness \nCentres \n135.000 00 135.000 \n22.  Training and Capacity building for \nAMO/MO/Consultants working in \nAYUSH Hospitals and Dispensaries \n10.000 00 10.000 \n23.  Admin Cost 139.900 00 139.900 \nTotal 6860.123 4841.500 11701.623 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n6 \n \nAnnexure-III \n \nThe State/UT-wise details of funds allocated/approved during the last five years and the current \nyear under NAM \n(Rs. in lakhs) \n \nSl. \nNo. \n \nName of \nStates/UTs \n2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nAllocated/ \nApproved  \nAllocated/ \nApproved  \nAllocated/ \nApproved  \nAllocated/ \nApproved  \nAllocated/ \nApproved  \nAllocated/ \nApproved  \n1.  Andaman & \nNicobar Islands \n302.330 317.886 365.418 251.808 296.061 284.783 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 1960.020 2133.677 3167.657 642.335 0.000 2186.080 \n3.  Arunachal \nPradesh \n606.340 608.754 425.922 753.433 200.944 1786.840 \n4.  Assam 2656.324 820.364 1779.247 385.722 710.578 4496.770 \n5.  Bihar 0.000 0.000 4435.497 860.892 2810.128 9311.916 \n6.  Chandigarh 490.520 52.552 0.000 195.808 94.864 758.938 \n7.  Chhattisgarh 2044.592 1778.113 0.000 4485.110 1402.182 4079.120 \n8.  Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli and \nDaman & Diu \n143.403 72.010 63.854 0.000 0.000 1208.770 \n9.  Goa 437.447 244.830 197.752 109.980 364.980 949.904 \n10.  Gujarat 2123.990 2934.758 3716.152 406.685 778.210 6362.085 \n11.  Haryana 1414.070 2002.268 3832.458 5057.370 1079.504 2710.900 \n12.  Himachal \nPradesh \n798.808 2118.900 2273.210 549.933 1402.000 8607.956 \n13.  Jammu & \nKashmir \n1102.871 1329.697 2053.932 2539.723 1513.105 7251.980 \n14.  Jharkhand 0.000 0.000 2537.186 0.000 2182.942 25841.898 \n15.  Karnataka 3433.110 4480.520 2986.182 3640.618 3035.767 5713.620 \n16.  Kerala 3493.723 4329.730 2567.558 3895.979 1922.313 9777.401 \n17.  Ladakh 0.000 0.000 0.000 0.000 187.454 289.060 \n18.  Lakshadweep 63.747 0.000 426.878 19.605 64.257 232.644 \n19.  Madhya Pradesh 5099.473 4715.210 6687.245 9347.721 5205.330 11441.777 \n20.  Maharashtra 2973.808 0.000 7181.072 0.000 0.000 5084.440 \n21.  Manipur 1488.140 1368.087 1007.861 635.244 188.933 7657.800 \n22.  Mizoram 770.531 803.043 221.678 730.810 287.870 521.200 \n23.  Meghalaya 820.282 0.000 386.899 275.522 677.533 3541.529 \n24.  Nagaland 1685.462 1023.188 1056.200 1394.322 258.078 2203.460 \n25.  Odisha 2601.700 1787.890 1210.160 1194.400 1792.300 5175.642 \n26.  Puducherry 239.995 445.520 238.416 79.445 333.788 2079.875 \n27.  Punjab 2247.772 62.421 1925.823 149.620 878.800 1305.320 \n28.  Rajasthan 11488.760 5036.957 3312.658 3794.000 5315.000 17055.073 \n29.  Sikkim 200.097 300.351 367.326 389.379 110.334 695.630 \n30.  Tamil Nadu 4648.453 3877.175 3323.188 1027.633 3913.780 11701.623 \n31.  Telangana 1758.521 1150.604 1556.968 0.000 5220.420 3847.270 \n32.  Tripura 1328.378 742.