    "ls|LS17_S11_U_299": " \nO.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 299 \n  ANSWERED ON 21/03/2023 \n \nREVIEW OF DEVELOPMENTAL WORKS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*299.  SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the status of various works like construction of school buildings, cattle shed, solid waste \nmanagement projects under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (MGNREGS); \n(b)  whether review of various developmental works like sports grounds, toilets, cleanliness, \npond cleaning, solid waste management, drinking water projects and prevention of \nencroachments has been done, if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the main focus of sanitation officers in villages is on self-assessment and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n(d)  whether the Government has given directions to all the departments that they should \nsubmit the progress report of their work to the office of Member of Parliament on time so \nthat the projects can be completed in a proper and time bound manner and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n  (a) to (d):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n   \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 299 for 21.03.2023 \n  \n \n(a)&(b):     Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is a demand driven wage employment Scheme. The core objective of the Scheme is to \nprovide not less than 100 days of unskilled manual work as a guaranteed employment i n a \nfinancial year to every household in rural areas as per demand, resulting in creation of productive \nassets of prescribed quality and durability.  \n           \nConstruction of school buildings is not permissible work under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS. Also, drin king water project and prevention of encroachments are the out of the \npreview of the Scheme.  \n \nSince inception of the Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, a total of   13.99 lakh cattle sheds are \nconstructed and 7.70 lakh cattle shed works are ongoing as on 16.03.2023. \n \nSince inception of the Scheme 16.64 lakh solid waste management works are completed \nand 2.66 lakh works are ongoing as on 16.03.2023. \n \nThe Ministry regularly reviews the performance of the implementation of Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS (including completion of ongo ing works) in States/UTs through various fora \nviz., Mid -Term Review, Labour Budget meetings, Labour Budget Revision meetings, \nProgramme Review meetings. Central Employment Guarantee Council and State Employment \nGuarantee Councils periodically monitor implementation of the programme. State Technical Cell \nare constituted at various level in the States to regularly monitor the progress of works.  \n  \n(c):    There is no such post of sanitation officer in the gram panchayat under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREG Scheme. \n \n(d):   No Sir. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_300": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT  \n \n LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.300 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2023 \n  \nWORKING WOMEN HOSTELS \n  \n \n300.     MS. NUSSRAT JAHAN: \n \n Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of working women hostels being operated by the Government in \nBasirhat district of West Bengal; \n(b) the details of all such facilities available in the said state, city-wise; \n(c) the manner in which the Government plans to expand such facilities; \n(d) the details of women enrolled and getting benefitted from such facilities during \nthe last five years; and \n(e) the total expenditure incurred by the Government on the said \nfacilities/scheme? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n   \n(a) & (b) As informed by the State Government of West Bengal, it does not implement \nany scheme on Working Women Hostel (WWH) in the State.   \n  \n(c) to (e) Under the newly approved Mission Shakti, the WWH known as Sakhi Niwas. \nThis is a demand driven scheme, meant to promote availability  of safe and \nconveniently located accommodation for working women, with day care facility for their \nchildren, in urban, semi -urban, or even rural areas where employment opportunity \nfor women exist, as per local needs and requirement of the States/UTs.   \n  \nDuring the last five years period expenditure for an amount of Rs.121.40 crores has \nbeen incurred under the Scheme i.e. Financial Years 2017-18 to 2021-22 with 23319 \nwomen beneficiaries.  \n  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_301": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN & CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 301 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2023 \n  \nIMPLEMENTATION OF ICDS PROGRAMME IN AWCS    \n  \n301:   SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government is taking any steps to ensure all children get access \nto Anganwadi Centres;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government has undertaken any study to analyse the actual \nimplementation of the  Integrated Child Development Scheme (ICDS) \nprogramme on the ground for children of migrant workers;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) whether the Government has any data about the number of children from age \n3 to 6 who are not enrolled in Anganwadis? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) & (b) Anganwadi Services is a self selecting universal scheme open to all eligible \nbeneficiaries on demand, irrespective of caste, religion and income criteria, who get \nthemselves registered at the nearest Anganwadi Centre. The scheme is administered \nand implemented by States/ UTs. Through sustained engagements at regular \nintervals, States/ UTs have been requested to optimize coverage of beneficiaries \nincluding child beneficiaries and ensure that children of appropriate age are enrolled \nat AWCs. Further, one major role entrusted on Anganwadi Worker is to elicit \ncommunity support and participation in running the programme. \n \n(c) & (d) NITI Aayog  had undertaken a study to evaluate the implementation of \nCentrally Sponsored Schemes in Women & Child Development Sector including \nAnganwadi Services Scheme. The study inter alia covered the availability of the \nvarious benefits to the migrant workers. In order to provide seamless distribution of \nSupplementary Nutrition to the migrant beneficiaries, migration facility has been made \navailable in the Poshan Tracker wherein in case the beneficiary is registered at \nanother AWC, she/he shall be migrated to the new AWC during the period of stay. \n \n(e) As per Census 2011, there are an estimated 16.45 crore children in the age-group \n0-6 years. As on 31st December 2022, 8.7 crore children in the age group 0 -6 years \nare registered as beneficiaries in Anganwadi Centres across the country, out of which, \nthe number of children aged 3 - 6 years, enrolled in Anganwadi Centres across the \ncountry, is 4.25 crores  \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_302": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 302 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3rd February, 2023 \n  \nJan Aushadhi Kendras in Andhra Pradesh  \n \n \n302. SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY:  \nSHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of Jan Aushadhi Kendras in Andhra Pradesh;  \n(b) the details of such stores in aspirational districts of the State;  \n(c) whether there are any proposals for opening up more Jan Aushadhi Kendras in the State;  \n(d) if so, the details and the current status thereof; and  \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n  \n(a) & (b): Under the Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) implemented \nby the Pharmaceutical and Medical Devices Bureau of India (PMBI ) under the aegis of the \nDepartment of Pharmaceuticals, till 31.12.2022, about 143 Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya \nJanaushadhi Kendras (PMBJKs) have been opened in the state of Andhra Pradesh, of which 12 \nPMBJKs have been opened in the aspirational districts of the State, as under:      \n         \nName of Aspirational District Number of PMBJKs \nVisakhapatanam 5 \nVizianagaram 4 \nY.S.R. 3 \nTotal 12 \n   \n(c) to (e) : As on 31.12.2022, about 9,000 PMBJKs have been opened across the country. A \ntarget of opening 10,000 PMBJKs across the country by March 2024 has been decided, but \nthere is no State specific target for opening of PMBJK.  In order to achieve the set target, PMBI \nhas recently invited online applications from 651 districts of the country, including all districts \nof Andhra Pradesh, except Nandyal district. \n \n***** ",
