    "ls|LS17_S11_U_333": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 333 \nANSWERED ON 23.03.2023 \n \nPRODUCTION OF SOLAR ENERGY \n*333. SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA \n          SHRI RAVI KISHAN \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has set a target to produce 200 GW of solar energy by 2024; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of the partial target(s) achieved so far in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.   \n \n***** \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT  \n  \nSTATEMENT REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 333 FOR 23.03.2023 REGARDING PRODUCTION OF SOLAR ENERGY \n \n(a)  & (b) Government has set a target of achieving 500 GW of installed capacity from non -fossil \nfuels, including around 270 GW of solar capacity, by 2030. \n(c) As on 28.02.2023, a cumulative solar power capa city of 64.38 GW has been installed in the \ncountry. In addition, solar projects of around 51.76 GW capacity are under implementation & \nanother 39.69 GW are under tendering stage.  \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_334": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 334 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 23 MARCH, 2023 \nCrude Oil Price \n \n†*334. SHRI DINESH CHANDRA YADAV: \nSHRI SANTOSH KUMAR: \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the price of crude oil in the international market has been stable for about 8-9 months \nand decreasing; \n(b) if so, the reasons for not providing Petrol, Diesel and Gas to public at low prices; and \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government to provide relief to the consumers? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \n( \u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह पुर\u0000 ) \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 334 \nasked by Shri Dinesh Chandra Yadav and Shri Santosh Kumar to be answered on 23 \nMarch 2023 regarding “Crude Oil Price”. \n \n(a) to (c): Prices of petrol and diesel have been market-determined with effect from \n26.06.2010 and 19.10.2014 respectively. Since then, the Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n(OMCs) take appropriate decision on pricing of petrol and diesel. Prices of petrol and diesel have \nnot been increased by public sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) since 6th April 2022. \n \nDue to high volatility and elevated prices of crude oil, most of the developed countries have \nbeen reeling under high Petrol and Diesel prices. As per data compiled by PPAC, in developed \ncountries like US, the prices of Petrol and Diesel increased by about 10.8% and 35.2% \nrespectively over the period January 2022 to January 2023, whereas during this period, prices in \nIndia (Delhi) increased only by 1.37% and 3.40 % for Petrol and Diesel respectively. Similarly, \nduring the same period significant increase in the prices of Petrol and Diesel has been seen in \nour neighbouring countries such as Pakistan (28% in Petrol and 40% in Diesel), Bangladesh \n(32% in Petrol and 24% in Diesel), Sri Lanka (29% in Petrol and 106% in Diesel) and in Nepal \n(31% in Petrol and 48% in Diesel). \n \nThe price of crude oil and petroleum products in the international markets fluctuate depending on \nvarious factors viz. demand-supply, geopolitical factors and other market conditions.  The \nmonthly average price of Indian Basket of Crude Oil for the last nine months are as under: \nMonth Indian Basket of Crude Oil ($/bbl) \nJune 2022 116.01 \nJuly 2022 105.49 \nAugust 2022 97.40 \nSeptember 2022 90.71 \nOctober 2022 91.70 \nNovember 2022 87.55 \nDecember 2022 78.10 \nJanuary 2023 80.92 \nFebruary 2023 82.28 \nSource: Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC) \nNote: The crude oil price in USD/Barrel terms does not reflect the depreciation of INR/USD exchange rate. \n \n \nThe Central Government has reduced Central Excise duty by a total of Rs. 13/litre and Rs. \n16/litre on petrol and diesel respectively in two tranches in November 2021 and May 2022. The \nmeasure was aimed to give a further fillip to the economy and to boost consumption and keep \ninflation low, thus helping the poor and middle classes.  \n \nIndia imports more than 60% of its domestic LPG consumption. Government continues to \nmodulate the effective price of domestic LPG. While the Saudi Contract Prices (CP), on which \ndomestic LPG prices are based, rose by 235 % from 236 $/MT in April 2020 to 790 $/MT in \nFebruary 2023, retail selling price of domestic LPG has increased only by 89.7 % from Rs. 581.5 \nin May 2020 to Rs.1103 in March 2023 and effective cost for PMUY rose only by 55.2 % during \nthe same period. Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies have suffered huge losses on sale of \ndomestic LPG. To compensate these losses, the Government has recently paid a one-time \ncompensation of Rs. 22000 crore to OMCs. W.e.f. 21.05.2022, Government has started a \ntargeted subsidy of Rs. 200 per 14.2 Kg cylinder for Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) \nbeneficiaries for upto 12 refills for year 2022-23. The Retail Selling Price (RSP) of domestic \nLPG at Delhi on 21.05.2022 was Rs. 1003 per 14.2 Kg cylinder. As on 01.03.2023, RSP of \ndomestic LPG at Delhi is Rs. 1103 per 14.2 Kg cylinder and effective price for PMUY \nconsumers is Rs. 903 per cylinder. \nx-x-x-x-x ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_335": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 335\n(To be answered on the 23rd March 2023)\n \nDEVELOPMENT OF INFRASTRUCTURE AT AIRPORTS IN TAMIL\nNADU\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has taken steps for the infrastructure\ndevelopment of Airports in Tamil Nadu and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government has also included the airports from other States\nalso under the Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS); and\n(c) if so, the details of the proposed plan for air connectivity with other cities?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n*****\n*335. DR. VISHNU PRASAD M.K.\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.\n335 REGARDING \"DEVELOPMENT OF INFRASTRUCTURE AT\nAIRPORTS IN TAMIL NADU\" TO BE ANSWERED ON 23.03.2023.\n \n(a) to (c): The Airports Authority of India has taken steps for\ndevelopment/upgradation of Airports in the State of Tamil Nadu namely\nChennai, Trichy, Madurai, Coimbatore and Tuticorin.\n \nBesides the above, Airports namely Salem, Neyveli, Vellore, Tanjore and\nRamnad  have been identified in the State of Tamil Nadu for flights under the\nUDAN (Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik) scheme. An airport which is included in the\nawarded routes of UDAN and requires upgradation/development for\ncommencement of UDAN operations, is developed under the 'Revival of\nunserved and underserved airports' scheme. Salem airport has been\noperationlized with UDAN flights under the scheme.\n \nThe Government has set a target to operationalize 1000 UDAN routes during\nthe currency of the scheme and to revive/develop 100 unserved & underserved\nairports/heliports/water aerodromes in the country by 2024 for operation of\nUDAN flights. As on 28.02.2023, the Selected Airline Operators (SAOs) have\noperationalized 469 routes connecting 74 (unserved/underserved) airports\nincluding 9 Heliports & 2 Water Aerodromes.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_336": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 336 \nANSWERED ON 23rd MARCH, 2023 \n \nPROBLEM OF TRAFFIC CONGESTION IN DELHI \n*336.  SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware of outer ring road traffic \ncongestion causing hardship to thousands of residents of West Enclave, \nRajya Sabha Society and Vidya Vihar, Pitampura in Delhi; \n(b) if so, the steps taken to widen the railway bridge on outer ring road \nto facilitate movement of traffic coming out of Mangolpuri towards \nPeera Garhi; \n(c) the steps taken to reduce traffic rush from Peera  Garhi crossing at \nNational Highway going to Rohtak, Haryana; and  \n(d) the steps taken to reduce traffic congestion in Delhi? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 336 ANSWERED ON 23.03.2023 ASKED \nBY SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL  REGARDING PROBLEM OF TRAFFIC \nCONGESTION IN DELHI \n(a) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways is primarily responsible \nfor development and maintenance of National Highways in the country. \nOuter ring road (ORR) is not a National Highway and is being developed \nand maintained by Government of National Capital Territory of Delhi \n(GNCTD).  \n(b)  As a part o f improvement to facilitate movement of traffic on ORR, \nwidening of Railway Bridge at Mangolpuri has been planned by GNCTD. \n \n(c)  This Ministry has taken up construction of spur to Bahadurgarh \nbypass, which is expected to reduce the traffic on section of NH-10 from \nPeera Garhi to Rohtak.  \n \n(d) This Ministry has taken up the comprehensive development of \nNational Highway network in National Capital at a cost of approximately \nRs 50,000 crore. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_337": "������������������� �\n����������������� �\n��������� �\n�������������������� ��� �\n������������ ���������� �\n�\n���� ����������������������������� �\n�\n���������������������������������� �\n�\n��������������������� ����� �\n�������������������� �\n�\n����� ���������������������������������������������� ���������������������������������\n������������������� ����������������������� �\n�\n����� �������� ������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� ��������������������������������������� �\n�\n���� ����������������������������������������� ������������������������������������\n���������� ������������������������������������������ ������������������������������� �\n�\n����� ���������������������������������������������� ���������������������������������\n������������������� ������������������������������� �\n�\n����������� �\n�\n������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n����������������� �\n�\n������������� ���������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������� �\n� �\n\n�\n�\n��������� �\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ �������� ��������� ���� ���� ���� ������ ��� ����������� ����������\n������������ ������� ��� ������ ����� ������� �� ������ ��������� ������\n����� �\n��������� �\n�\n��������� ������������������������ ������� ������������������������ ����������� �\n�������� ����� ���� � ����� � ���������� ����� ����� � ������� ����������\n����������� ��������������������������������� ������ ������������������\n������� ����� ��� ��������� �� ����� ����� ��� ����� ����� ��� �������� ����\n��������������������������������������������������������� ��������������� �\n�\n���� ���� ������� ���� ���� ������ ������������ ������ ����� �������\n����� ����� ���� �������� ���������� ����� ������� ����� ������ ������ ���������\n� �����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ����� ���� �������� �������� ���� ����