    "ls|LS17_S11_U_338": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION No-338\nANSWERED ON-03/02/2023\nDIFFICULTIES IN VISA APPOINTMENTS\n338. SHRIMATI SARMISTHA SETHI\n        SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU\nWill the Minister of External Affairs be pleased to state :-\n(a) whether the Government has noticed that students are facing difficulties in obtaining visas\nfor higher education to the United States due to unavailability of appointments and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(b)  whether  the  Government  is  aware  that  such  students  are  losing  their  admissions  in\nprestigious universities due to unavailability of visa appointments;\n(c) whether the Ministry has a portal or mechanism in place that the students can contact in\ncase they face difficulties in obtaining an appointment;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, reasons therefor;\n(e) whether the Ministry has taken up the issue of difficulty in obtaining visas for higher\neducation overseas with the US Government; and\n(f) if so, the details of steps being taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI V . MURALEEDHARAN)\n(a to f) The Ministry is aware of challenges faced by Indian students for obtaining visas for\nUSA in the last two years, particularly in wake of the COVID-19 pandemic. The matter has\nbeen taken up at high levels with the U.S. Government, including at Ministerial meetings.\nGrant of visa is the sovereign decision and prerogative of a foreign government, acting\nthrough their Missions, based on their procedures.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_339": " \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No.*339 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 23rd March, 2023 \n \nPiped Cooking Gas Supply \n \n*339. SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to connect all households with piped cooking gas in the cities/major \ncities in the country and if so, the details thereof, State/UT and city-wise; \n(b) the details of the cities in various parts of the country where Piped Natural Gas (PNG) facility has \nbeen made available for cooking, State/UT and district-wise, particularly in Maharashtra State, district-\nwise; \n(c) the number of cities to be provided/proposed with gas supply facility under PNG during the next two \nyears, the State/UT and district-wise details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government proposes to implement the scheme in the city of Lucknow and Noida as well \nand if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether the Government has received complaints of irregularities in the reading meter of piped \ncooking gas in the country and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f) whether it is a fact that piped cooking gas is more cheaper than LPG cylinder and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह पुर\u0000  )  \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n  \n(a) to (f) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *339 ASKED BY SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE, MP TO BE \nANSWERED ON 23rd MARCH, 2023. \n(a) to (d) Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) has authorized approximately 33,764 \nkm length of Natural Gas Pipeline Network across the Country with the aim to create a national gas grid \nand increase the availability of natural gas across the Country. As on 31.12.2022, approximately 22,335 \nkm pipeline is operational and 12,955 kms of pipeline is under construction.  \nProviding Piped Natural Gas (PNG) connections to the households are part of development of City Gas \nDistribution (CGD) Network and the same is carried out by the entities authorized by PNGRB. After \ncompletion of 11-A bidding round, 297 Geographical Areas (GAs) covering about 98% of the population, \nincluding slum dwellers and people living below the poverty line falling under these GAs, and 88% of \ntotal geographical area of the country spread over around 630 districts in 28 States/UTs,  have been \nauthorized by PNGRB for development of CGD Network. The PNG(D) connections have increased from \nabout 25 lakhs in 2014 to about 65 lakhs in 2020 and further to more than 1 crore in 2022. The total \nlength of CGD network laid (both steel and MDPE ) rose from 1.38 lakh inch kms in 2018 to 3.74 lakh \ninch kms in March 2022 and further to 4.27 lakh inch kms in December 2022. \nPNG connections are provided as per the Minimum Work Programme (MWP) mandated by PNGRB and \ntechno-commercial feasibility.  As per the MWP, after completion of 11-A bidding round, CGD entities \nhave to provide 12.33 crore PNG connections across the country by 2030.  As on 31.01.2023, 1.056 crore \nPNG connections have been provided by the CGD entities. \n(e) Individual complaints received from time to time relating to delay in meter installation, default meter \nreading etc. are referred to the concerned authorised entity for suitable redressal at their end.  Under \nPNGRB Act, the Board has jurisdiction to receive any complaint from any person and can conduct any \ninquiry and investigation connected with the activities relating to petroleum, petroleum products and \nnatural gas on contravention of retail service obligations, marketing service obligations and display of \nretail price at retail outlets. Any complaint received by PNGRB is disposed as per the provisions of the \nAct and regulations framed thereunder. \n(f) Current Prices of PNG and LPG are listed below:  \nPrice as on 01.03.2023 (Delhi) LPG  PNG  \nLPG per cylinder/ PNG per SCM 1103.00  53.59 \nIn Rs/Million Kcal  68.7 53.5 \nIn terms of calorific value of different petroleum products, current prices and Rs/million calories, PNG is \ngenerally cheaper per unit of energy consumed as compared to LPG.  Depending upon local prices, \nconversion factor of the cooking stove, etc., PNG provides a positive competitive advantage over LPG. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_340": "Ls usq No.340GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF CHEMICALS AND PETROCHEMICALS \n  \n���������  \n��������������������������  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03/02/2023 \n  \n������������������������������������  \n  \n340. SHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA \n  \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)     whether it is a fact that the enrollments, assessments and success rate under the \nskill development programme of the Central Institute of Petrochemicals Engineering \nand Technology (CIPET) has declined in the past three years; \n            (b)     if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor, year-wise; \n(c)      whether the Government has taken any steps to increase the enrollments, \nassessments and success rate; and  \n(d)       if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \n������  \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS (SHRI BHAGWANTH \nKHUBA) \n� \n(a)       Central Institute of Petrochemicals Engineering  and Technology (CIPET) \nregularly conducts the Skill Development Training Programmes with the support of \nvarious Ministries / State Skill Development Missions / Govt. Departments / PSUs / \nPrivate Organizations for the benefit of underprivileged / unemploye d youths of their \nrespective target group / beneficiaries. The no. of beneficiaries benefited in the skill \ndevelopment training programme at CIPET centres during last three years is as follows: \n  \n������� \n  \n�������������� \n���������������������������� \n������������������� \n�������� ���������  \n1. 2020-21 29,581 29,465 (99.60%) \n2. 2021-22 34,208 34,027 (99.47%) \n3. \n2022-23 \n(upto December \n2022) \n32,267 32,153 (99.64%) \n  \n� \n\n(b)        Due to the impact of COVID -19 pandemic, the skill development training \nprogrammes were severely hampered during the period from March 2020 to September \n2021. Now the enrollment is gradually improving.  \n  \n            (c)        Yes. \n  \n(d)       From the year 2022 -23, CIPET has again started receiving sanctions from \nvarious Ministries / State Skill Development Mission / Govt. Department / PSUs / \nPrivate Organization for conducting skill development training programmes and all \nCIPET centres are making concerted efforts for enrolling more number of candidates \nin the training programme followed by assessment of admitted candidates in the \ntraining programme. In view of above, the number of student enrollment in skill \ndevelopment training programmes is gradually increasing at CIPET. \n  \n  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_341": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 341 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 24TH MARCH, 2023 \n \nCASES OF LEPROSY \n \n*341. PROF. SOUGATA RAY:  \n          SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the reports that 52 per cent leprosy \ncases of world belong to India and if so, the details thereof indicating the  estimated \nleprosy patients at present in the country, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has any strategic roadmap to eliminate leprosy from the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether leprosy cases in India are declining fast durin g the last few years and if \nso, the details thereof indicating the proportionate decline thereof;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has fixed a target of Leprosy Mukt Bharat under \n‘National Leprosy Eradication Programme’ by 2027 and if so, the details thereof and \nthe steps taken to achieve the set target; and  \n \n(e) the details of funds sanctioned and released  by the Government to achieve \nLeprosy Mukt Bharat by 2027? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to ( e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 341 FOR 24 TH MARCH, 2023 \n \n(a): India has achieved elimination of leprosy as a public health problem as per WHO \ncriteria of less than 1 case per 10,000 population at the National level in 2005.With \nvarious interventions introduced under National Leprosy Eradication Programme \n(NLEP) in the last few years, number of new leprosy cases detected have come down \nto 75,394 in 2021 -22 from 1,25,785 in 2014 -15. The details of number of leprosy \ncases found in the country, State/UT-wise is at Annexure-I. \n(b) to (d): The Government of India has l aunched National Strategic Plan (NSP) & \nRoadmap for Leprosy (2023 -27) on 30 th January, 2023, to achieve zero transmission \nof leprosy by 2027 i.e. three years before the Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) \n3.3. The NSP contains implementation strategies, tar gets, public health approaches \nand overall technical guidance for the programme. The strategy and roadmap focuses \non awareness for zero stigma & discrimination, promotion of early case detection, \nprevention of disease transmission by prophylaxis (Leprosy P ost Exposure \nProphylaxis) and roll out of web -based information portal (Nikusth 2.0) for reporting \nof leprosy cases. \nThe Prevalence Rate (PR) at national level has decreased to 0.45 per 10,000 \npopulation in 2021 -22 as compared to 0.69 per 10,000 population  in 2014-15.  The \ndetails of proportionate change in leprosy cases for last three years are at Annexure-\nII. \n(e):  The details of State/UT -wise Central Release for NLEP under National Health \nMission (NHM) during the last three years is at Annexure-III. \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure- I  \n \nNumber of leprosy cases in the country (State/UT-wise) \n \nState/UT Number of Cases (Till Jan'2023) \nAndhra Pradesh 2769 \nArunachal Pradesh 10 \nAssam 719 \nBihar 11318 \nChhattisgarh 7422 \nGoa 70 \nGujarat 2662 \nHaryana 366 \nHimachal Pradesh 132 \nJharkhand 6184 \nJammu & Kashmir 106 \nKarnatka 2091 \nKerala 384 \nMadhya Pradesh 7313 \nMaharashtra 17014 \nManipur 17 \nMeghalaya 6 \nMizoram 2 \nNagaland 31 \nOdisha 6088 \nPunjab 365 \nRajasthan 969 \nSikkim 15 \nTamil Nadu 2591 \nTelangana 2564 \nTripura 18 \nUttar Pradesh 10312 \nUttrakhand 294 \nWest Bengal 5012 \nA & N Island 5 \nChandigarh 141 \nD & N Haveli 76 \nDaman & Diu 2 \nDelhi 1188 \nLakshadweep 0 \nLadakh 8 \nPuducherry 14 \n \n \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure- II  \n \n \nTrends of new leprosy cases in India \n \nYear Prevalence Rate (Under-treatment \ncases/10,000 po