    "ls|LS17_S11_U_359": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF LEGAL AFFAIRS  \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.359 \nANSWERED ON 03/02/2023 \n  \nSETTING UP OF TRIBUNALS \n  \n359. SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n  \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the number of tribunals set up in the country under Article 323 -A and B \nof the Constitution, State-wise; \n  \n(b) the number of tribunals set up following legislations or any other \nmethod/treaty/agreement, State-wise; \n  \n(c ) the number of tribunals pre sently functioning and those discontinued, \nState-wise;  \n  \n(d) the number of tribunals with vacancies, State-wise; and  \n  \n(e) the total expenditure incurred by the Government year -wise over the last \nfive years in operating such tribunals?  \n  \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW & JUSTICE \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n  \n(a) to (e): As per Tribunals Reforms Act, 2021, there are sixteen functional \nTribunals, which are administered under respective Acts by different \nMinistries/Departments/State Governments. Under Article 323A a nd 323B of \nthe Constitution, the State Governments are also empowered to set up Tribunals \nfor adjudicating disputes in the respective States. The Central Government is \nnot mandated to administer the Tribunals, set up by the State Governments and \nhence no d ata on State wise Tribunals and expenses incurred on administering \nthem is maintained by the Central Government.  \n  \nAs per Finance Act, 2017 and the Tribunals Reforms Act, 2021, thirteen \nTribunals have since been discontinued by the Central Government.  \n  \n ***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_360": "  \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 360 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24th March, 2023 \n  \nSelection of Medicines under PMBJP  \n \n*360. SHRI JAYANT SINHA:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has adopted any methodology for selection of medicines and \nsurgical instruments in the product basket under the Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi \nPariyojana (PMBJP);  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the details of the category of medicines included in the said scheme and the categories being \nconsidered for subsequent expansion of the product basket;  \n(d) the rationale adopted for the differential discounting of the categories  of Jan Aushadhi \nmedicines; and  \n(e) the number of functioning PMBJP kendras, State -wise, district-wise including Jharkhand \nand year-wise since 2016-17? \n \n ANSWER \n  \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (Dr. MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n  \n(a)  to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED Q.  NO. 360 (20 TH POSITION) FOR ANSWER ON 24.03.2023 \nRAISED BY SHRI JAYANT SINHA REGARDING SELECTION OF MEDICINES \nUNDER PMBJP \n  \nThe product basket of Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana  (PMBJP) \nconsists of 1,759 medicines and 280 surgical devices as on 28.02.2023. Based on feedback of \nvarious stakeholders and market demand, the product basket is regularly reviewed and \nexpanded. An expert committee has also been constituted, which meets p eriodically, review \nthe product basket and recommend inclusion of new products. \n \nThe product basket of PMBJP  presently covers all major therapeutic groups such as \nCardiovascular, Anti-cancers, Anit-diabetics, Anti-infectives, Anti-allergic, Gastro-intestinal \nmedicines, Nutraceuticals, etc.  In addition, various nutraceutical products like Protein Powder, \nMalt-based Food Supplements, etc. and some AYUSH products like Ayuraksha Kit, Balraksha \nKit and Ayush -64 tablet and Chyawanprash as Immunity boosters have been added in the \nproduct basket of the Pariyojana. These medicines and surgical devices are available for sale \nthrough Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras (PMBJKs) across the country.  \n \nUnder the scheme, a medicine is priced based on principle of maximum of 50% of the \naverage price of the top three branded medicines. Thus, the price of medicines under the \nscheme are cheaper at least by 50% and in some cases by 80% to 90% of market price of the \nbranded medicines. \n \nThe list of number of State/UT - wise and financial year -wise PMBJKs opened across \nthe country including in the state of Jharkhand since 2016 -17 is enclosed as  Annexure-I. \nDistrict-wise list of PMBJKs opened as on 28.02.2023 across the country is enclosed as \nAnnexure-II.  \n  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nFinancial Year-wise and State/UT-wise PMBJKs opened in the country since 2016-17 \nSl. \nNo. \n Name of the \nState/UT \nFY 2016-\n17 \nFY 2017-\n18 \nFY 2018-\n19 \nFY 2019-\n20 \nFY 2020-\n21 \nFY 2021-\n22 \nFY 2022-23 till \n28.02.2023 \n1 Andaman & \nNicobar 0 0 2 0 0 7 0 \n2 Andhra \nPradesh 25 50 35 11 10 5 10 \n3 Arunachal \nPradesh 13 9 0 6 0 0 1 \n4 Assam 11 17 20 16 11 15 20 \n5 Bihar 3 85 44 29 62 63 72 \n6 Chandigarh 1 0 1 1 1 0 2 \n7 Chhattisgarh 28 27 7 8 17 12 4 \n8 Delhi 4 25 35 67 157 81 11 \n9 Goa 0 0 8 0 1 1 1 \n10 Gujarat 58 129 127 70 76 44 15 \n11 Haryana 8 44 45 29 38 37 28 \n12 Himachal \nPradesh 3 11 16 10 6 6 2 \n13 Jammu and \nKashmir 4 13 21 38 5 38 92 \n14 Jharkhand 7 23 10 13 5 9 8 \n15 Karnataka 22 218 182 167 257 99 104 \n16 Kerala 127 139 119 95 229 247 18 \n17 Ladakh 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 \n18 Lakshadeep * 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n19 Madhya \nPradesh 15 34 63 54 46 26 33 \n20 Maharashtra 40 121 128 127 121 59 33 \n21 Manipur 0 28 0 0 1 3 3 \n22 Meghalaya 0 1 0 8 5 1 1 \n23 Mizoram 0 3 3 6 0 0 0 \n24 Nagaland 11 0 3 1 0 4 1 \n25 Odisha 4 35 66 66 74 88 49 \n26 Puducherry 0 11 3 2 0 3 1 \n27 Punjab 1 43 60 60 84 29 15 \n28 Rajasthan 10 38 25 16 16 29 14 \n29 Sikkim 0 2 0 0 1 0 1 \n30 Tamil Nadu 28 218 207 112 163 97 47 \n31 Telangana 14 51 32 14 26 22 19 \n32 The D&NH \nand D&D 0 7 5 6 12 5 0 \n33 Tripura 1 13 2 1 0 0 1 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 77 334 291 127 194 135 165 \n35 Uttarakhand 15 67 64 31 24 15 7 \n36 West Bengal 3 48 38 33 36 37 42 \nTotal 533 1844 1662 1225 1678 1217 820 \n  \n\n Annexure-II \n  \nState/ District wise list of PMBJKs opened across the country till 28.02.2023  \nState Name District Name No. of PMBJKs \nAndaman And Nicobar Islands Nicobars 3 \n  North And Middle Andaman 3 \n  South Andamans 3 \nAndhra Pradesh Alluri Sitharama Raju 3 \n  Anakapalli 4 \n  Anantapur 8 \n  Annamayya 5 \n  Bapatla 3 \n  Chittoor 6 \n  East Godavari 4 \n  Eluru 6 \n  Guntur 11 \n  Kakinada 2 \n  Konaseema 5 \n  Krishna 2 \n  Kurnool 10 \n  Nandyal 12 \n  Ntr 7 \n  Palnadu 9 \n  Parvathipuram Manyam 1 \n  Prakasam 6 \n  Spsr Nellore 4 \n  Sri Sathya Sai 4 \n  Srikakulam 3 \n  Tirupati 8 \n  Visakhapatanam 5 \n  Vizianagaram 4 \n  West Godavari 11 \n  Y.S.R. 3 \nArunachal Pradesh Anjaw 1 \n  Changlang 1 \n  Dibang Valley 1 \n  East Kameng 1 \n  East Siang 2 \n  Kamle 1 \n  Kra Daadi 2 \n  Kurung Kumey 1 \n  Leparada 1 \n  Lohit 1 \n  Longding 1 \n  Lower Dibang Valley 2 \n  Lower Siang 1 \n  Lower Subansiri 1 \n  Namsai 1 \n  Pakke Kessang 1 \n  Papum Pare 2 \n  Shi Yomi 1 \n  Siang 1 \n  Tawang 1 \n\n  Tirap 1 \n  Upper Siang 1 \n  Upper Subansiri 1 \n  West Kameng 1 \n  West Siang 1 \nAssam Baksa 1 \n  Barpeta 6 \n  Biswanath 1 \n  Bongaigaon 3 \n  Cachar 19 \n  Charaideo 1 \n  Chirang 1 \n  Darrang 4 \n  Dhemaji 2 \n  Dhubri 2 \n  Dibrugarh 2 \n  Dima Hasao 1 \n  Goalpara 1 \n  Golaghat 2 \n  Hailakandi 5 \n  Hojai 2 \n  Jorhat 1 \n  Kamrup 7 \n  Kamrup Metro 12 \n