    "ls|LS17_S11_U_386": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \n���������� �\n�������������������������� �\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2023 \n  \n����������������������������������������������������������������������  \n�\n386:  SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE: \n� \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether shelter homes for women and children currently offer mental health \ncounselling in the country;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the number of such homes, State/UT-wise;  \n(c)  whether there exists any monitoring protocol to ensure that mental health \ncounselling is provided in all shelter homes; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n�\n������ �\n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b)  The Ministry of Women and Child Development implements various \nSchemes which have the provision of shelter homes for providing medical care, \nincluding mental health counseling, to the women and children who are in difficult \ncircumstances or are without any  social and economic support. The details of such \nSchemes are given below:  \n  \n������������ : Under the newly approved Mission Shakti, Swadhar Greh for women \nin difficult circumstances and Ujjawala Homes for Prevention of Trafficking have been \nmerged and renamed as Shakti Sadan which is an Integrated Relief and Rehabilitation \nHome. This is aimed at creating a safe and enabling environment for the women in \ndistress situations and difficult circumstances.   State/UT-wise list of functional Shakti \nSadans (Swadhar Grehs and Ujjawala Homes) is at �������� ���  \n  \n�������� ��������� Mission Vatsalya Scheme under which support is provided to \nStates and UT Governments for delivering services for children in need and in difficult \ncircumstances.  The Child Care Institutions  (CCIs) established under the scheme \nsupport inter-alia age-appropriate education, access to vocational training, recreation, \nhealth care, counselling etc. and covers both rural and urban children.   The scheme \nsupports 24x7 outreach helpline services for c hildren in distress. This service is \navailable through a dedicated toll free number, 1098 which can be accessed by \nchildren in crisis or by adults on their behalf from any place in the geographical location \nof India. The number of shelter homes/CCIs (as on 31.03.2022) is at �������� ���. \n  \n\n(c) & (d)    Under Section 53 of the JJ Act, 2015 (as amended in 2021) provides for \nsetting up of Management Committee in every CCIs to manage the institution and \nmonitor the progress of every child. The JJ Model Rules als o provide for constitution \nof Management Committees in every institution for the management of the institution \nand monitoring the progress of every child. The Management Committees are required \nto set-up a complaint and redressal mechanism in every CCI. A Suggestion Box for \nchildren is to be placed at a place easily accessible to children, away from the office \nset-up and closer to the residence or rooms or dormitories of the children. \n  \nFurther, in 2020, the Ministry of Women and Child Development in collaboration \nwith National Institute of Mental Health & Neurosciences (NIMHANS), Bengaluru \nestablished Support, Advocacy & Mental Health Interventions for Children in \nVulnerable Circumstances and Distress (SAMVAD), a national initiative & integrated \nresource for child protection, mental health, & psychosocial care, that provides training \nand capacity building and other forms of guidance and technical supports, to child \nprotection functionaries who work in children’s shelter home across the country. \nSAMVAD’s training equips child protection functionaries with knowledge and skills in \nchild interviewing and inquiry (embedded in contexts of risk and vulnerability), \nidentification of and first level responses to common emotional, assistance at \nsecondary and tertiary mental healthcare facilities. As per information received from \nNIMHANS, SAMVAD has reached 5,419 child protection functionaries in 14 States \nand 2 Union Territories across 410 Districts. \n  \nThe Ministry of Women and Child Development implements the com ponent of \nOne Stop Centre under which integrated services such as Medical aid, Psycho-social \ncounseling, Police facilitation, Legal aid and counseling etc. provided under one roof \nto the needy and distressed women. The Ministry of Women and Child Developme nt \nthrough National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences (NIMHANS) has also \nprovided basic and advanced training under the project named ‘Stree Manoraksha’ to \nthe staff of One Stop Centres (OSCs) across the country on  handling psycho-social \nand mental health care needs to support the women facing violence and women in \ndistress. \n  \nBesides this the Hub for Empowerment of Women (HEW) are envisaged at \nNational, State and District levels for monitoring of activities. \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n\n�������� ���\n \n��������� ����� ������� ��� ������ ��� ������ ���� �� ���� ��� ���� ������\n���������� ��������� ���� ���� ���� ������� ��� ����������� ����������\n������� ������� ������������ ��� �������� ������ ������ ��� �����\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n  \nState/UT-wise list of functional Shakti Sadans (Swadhar Grehs and Ujjawala Homes) \n  \n  \n� \n� \n�\n�� \n��� \n�������������  �������������  \n����������  \n��������������  \n����������  \n���������������  \n1. � Andaman & Nicobar Islands 1 0 \n2. � Andhra Pradesh 21 5 \n3. � Arunachal Pradesh 1 0 \n4. � Assam 17 19 \n5. � Chandigarh 1 0 \n6. � Chhattisgarh 2 2 \n7. � Delhi 2 0 \n8. � Goa 2 1 \n9. � Gujarat 6 0 \n10. � Himachal Pradesh 1 0 \n11. � Jammu & Kashmir 2 0 \n12. � Jharkhand 5 2 \n13. � Karnataka 51 14 \n14. � Kerala 7 2 \n15. � Madhya Pradesh 15 0 \n16. � Maharashtra 10 4 \n17. � Manipur 23 22 \n18. � Meghalaya 2 0 \n19. � Mizoram 11 1 \n20. � Nagaland 2 0 \n21. � Odisha 52 16 \n22. � Punjab 2 0 \n23. � Rajasthan 8 1 \n24. � Sikkim 1 0 \n25. � Tamil Nadu 35 0 \n26. � Telangana 18 2 \n27. � Tripura 3 0 \n28. � Uttar Pradesh 13 0 \n  Uttrakhand 0 2 \n      30. West Bengal 33 1 \n� ����� ���  �������������� \n\n�������� ����\n \n��������� ����� ������� ��� ������ ��� ������ ���� �� ���� ��� ���� ������\n���������� ��������� ���� ���� ���� ������� ��� ����������� ����������\n������� ������� ������������ ��� �������� ������ ������ ��� �����\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ���� ��� �������� ����������� ��������� ������� �������� ��������� �������\n������������������������������������������������������������������� \n� \n���\n��� ����� \n��������������  �������������  ������������\n������������������\n�����  \n������������ ������������ ������������ \n1 Andhra Pradesh 56 5 15 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 5 0 2 \n3 Assam 35 5 18 \n4 Bihar 31 0 25 \n5 Chhattisgarh 42 9 12 \n6 Goa 15 2 2 \n7 Gujarat 59 3 13 \n8 Haryana 25 12 6 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 30 4 1 \n10 Jammu and Kashmir 11 0 2 \n11 Jharkhand 24 2 9 \n12 Karnataka 74 35 36 \n13 Kerala 19 3 8 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 46 9 27 \n15 Maharashtra 22 13 17 \n16 Manipur 47 17 9 \n17 Meghalaya 37 4 4 \n18 Mizoram 32 0 6 \n19 Nagaland 23 2 4 \n20 Odisha 83 12 27 \n21 Punjab 13 0 6 \n22 Rajasthan 60 20 33 \n23 Sikkim 14 3 3 \n24 Tamil Nadu 182 11 21 \n25 Tripura 19 4 6 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 38 13 24 \n27 Uttarakhand 9 4 5 \n28 West Bengal 62 38 26 \n29 Telangana 37 0 14 \n30 Andaman & Nicobar 10 0 1 \n31 Chandigarh 5 0 2 \n32 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and \nDaman & Diu 2 1 1 \n33 Ladakh 0 0 0 \n34 Lakshadweep 1 0 0 \n35 National Capital Territory \nof Delhi 24 9 3 \n36 Puducherry 23 1 2 \n   ����� ����  ���  ���  \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_387": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n38\n7\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n28\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2023\n \n \nMIDH SCHEME IN ANDHRA PRADESH\n \n \n*\n38\n7\n.\n \n \nDR. BEESETTI VENKATA SAT\nYAVATHI\n:\n \n \nWill the \nMinister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष और \u0000कसान क\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी \nbe\n \npleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe goals of the Mission for Integrated\n \nDevelopment of Horticulture (MIDH) \nscheme;\n \n(b) \n \nwhether it is a fact that Andhra Pradesh has\n \nwitnessed a mere Rs. 18 crore \nexpen\nditure under the\n \nscheme in 2020\n-\n2\n021;\n \n(c) \n \nif so, the reasons therefor; and\n \n(d) \n \nthe details of activities undertaken with this\n \namount for the financial year 2020\n-\n21 \nalong with the\n \nfollow up done with regard to progress made and its\n \nsustainability?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष और \u0000कसान क\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n       \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (\nd\n):\n \n     \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEME\nNT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (\nd\n) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION \nNO. \n3\n87\n \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON 28.\n0\n3\n.202\n3\n \nREGARDING\n \nMI\nDH SCHEME \nIN ANDHRA PRADESH\n.\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \n \nGovernment is implementing Mission for Integrated Development of H\norticulture \n(MIDH),\n \na Centrally Sponsored Scheme for holistic growth of the horticulture sector in the \ncount\nry\n \nresulting\n \nin increase of horti\nculture produ\nction fr\nom 277.35 million \nMT in 2013\n-\n14 \nto 342.32 millio\nn MT\n \n(3\nrd\n \nadva\nnce estimates)\n \nin 2021\n-\n22 in the country\n. \nThe major \ngoals/objectives of MIDH scheme are as under:\n \n \n\n \nPromote holistic growth of horticulture s\nector, inclu\nding bamboo \nand coconut \nthrough ar\nea based\n \nregionally differentiated strategies, which includes research, \ntechnology promotion, extension, pos\nt\n-\nharvest management, processing and \nmarketing, in consonance with comparative advantage of each\n \nState\n/region and \nits diverse \nagro\n-\nclimatic features\n;\n \n\n \nEncourage aggregation of farmers into farmer groups like FIGs/FPOs and FPCs to \nbring economy of scale\n \nand \nscope.\n \n\n \nEnhance horticulture production, augment farmers, income and strengthen \nnutritional security;\n \n\n \nI\nmprove pr\noductivity by w\nay of quality germplas\nm, planting material and water use \nefficiency through\n \nMicro Irrigation.\n \n\n \nSupport skill development and create\n \nemployment generation opportunities for \nrural youth in horticulture\n \nand post harvest management, espe\ncially in\n \nthe cold \nchain\n \nsector.\n \n \nAchievements\n \nof\n \nmajor components under MIDH \nduring\n \n2014\n-\n15 to 202\n2\n-\n23\n \nare\n \na\ns \nunder: \n \n \n\n \nArea Expansion\n: An additional area\n \nof \n12.16\n \nLakh ha under horticulture crops \nhas been covered.\n \n\n \nNurseries\n: \n-\n \n807\n \nnurseries have been esta\nblished f\nor production o\nf quality \nplanting mat\nerial.\n \n\n \nRejuvenation\n: An ar\nea of 1.3\n5\n \nLakh ha. has been covered under rejuvenation of \nold and senile orchards\n.\n \n\n \nProtected Cultivation\n: An area of 2.\n64\n \nLakh ha has been covered under \nprotected cultivation.\n \n\n \nWater R\nesources\n:\n \n-\n   \n4\n7101\n \nwate\nr harvesting structure\ns have been created\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \nHorticulture Mechanization\n: 2.4\n9\n \nLakh horticulture mechanization equipments \nhave been di\nstributed.\n \n\n \nBeekeeping\n: 15.\n68\n \nLakh bee colonies with hives have been distributed.\n \n\n \nPost Harvest Manageme\nnt Infras\ntructures\n: Unde\nr the component of \nPost \nHarvest Management\n, \n103634\n \nunits including pack houses, cold storage units, \nrefrigerated vans, primary/ mo\nb\nile processing units, ripening chambers, pre \ncooling units attach to cold storages \nand mobile\n \npre co\noling unit\ns have been \nes\ntablished.\n \n\n \nMarket In\nfrastructures\n:\n \n1\n2904\n \nma\nrket infrastructures have been set up. \n \n\n \nTraining of Farmers\n: Under HRD component, 8.\n71\n \nL\nakh farmers have been \ntrained various horticulture activities.\n \n \n(b) \nto\n \n(\nd\n)\n:\n \n \nDuring 2020\n-\n21, State Hort\nicu\nlture Mission (SHM) A\nndhra Pradesh has \nreported an expenditure of Rs. \n83.27\n \ncror\ne \nunder MIDH scheme.\n \nD\netails of activities \nunder\ntaken with this amount\n \nare as under:\n \nRs. in Lakhs\n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nComponent \n \nUnit \n \nAchievement\n \nPhysical \n \nFinancial \n \n1\n \nPlantation\n \n& \nInfrastructure Develo\npment\n \nNo.\n \n1\n \n7.50\n \n2\n \nEstablishment o\nf new gardens \n \nHa. \n \n12301\n \n2263.03\n \n3\n \nII Year maintenance 2019\n-\n20 \n \nHa. \n \n1\n0070\n \n537.90\n \n4\n \nIII Year\n \nmaintenance 2018\n-\n19\n \nHa. \n \n4784\n \n193.95\n \n5\n \nRejuvenation\n \nHa. \n \n2418\n \n423.15\n \n6\n \nCreation of Water resources \n \nNo\n. \n \n237\n \n793.75\n \n7\n \nProt\nected cultivation \n \nHa. \n \n1853\n \n313.59\n \n8\n \nIntegrated Post Harvest Management \n \nNo.\n \n436\n \n1512.37\n \n9\n \nEstablishment\n \nof Marketing Infrastru\ncture\n \nNo.\n \n28\n \n175.25\n \n10\n \nPollination support through bee keeping\n \nNo.\n \n514\n \n4.83\n \n11\n \nHorticulture Mechaniz\nati\non\n \nNo.\n \n3660\n \n612.02\n \n1\n2\n \nHuman Resource D\nevelopment\n \nNo.\n \n15\n190\n \n151.90\n \n13\n \nTechnology Dissemination through \ndemonstration/FLD\n \n \n \n6\n \n15\n0.00\n \n14\n \nInformation di\nssemination through\n \npublicity, printed literature etc and local\n \nadvertisements\n \n \n \n100\n \n40.00\n \n15\n \nDevelop\nmen\nt of technology packa\nges in electronic \nform to be shared\n \nthrough IT Network \n-\n \n(1 \nlakh/district)\n \n \n \n50\n \n50.00\n \n\n \n \n16\n \nTechnical support\n \ngroup experts/staff \nmo\nnitoring/publicity/video conference/ (project based) \nIT Development\n \n \n \n2\n \n50.00\n \n17\n \nSpecial initiatives \n(Chilli value chain deve\nlopment, \nBanana\n \ncluster development\n. Spices value chain \ndevelopment etc.)\n \n \n \n \n \n47.94\n \n18\n \nMission Management\n \n \n \n \n \n1000.00\n \n \n \nTotal\n \n \n \n \n \n8327.18\n \n \nImplementation of the above\n-\nmentioned activities has resulted in increase in \nthe productiv\nity, disease resistance \nof horticulture crops, good remuner\native prices of \ncrops during off season, \nreduction \nin\n \nPost\n-\nHarvest losse\ns\n, increase in \nhorticul\ntu\nre \nexport \nspecially in\n \nChilli \n&\n \nBanana\n \nand ultimately\n \nenha\nncement in\n \nthe\n \noverall\n \nincome \nof the farme\nrs\n.\n \n \nF\nor better implemen\ntation of\n \nthe scheme, p\nrogress of MIDH is monitored by \nGovernment of India\n \nthrough review meetings and fi\nel\nd visits\n. Accounts of S\nHMs are \nalso audited every year to track expenditure under the Mission. Web based progress \nmonitoring\n \ns\nystem is\n \nalso\n \nin place \nwherein physical and financial progress is \nuploaded on a monthly basis.\n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_388": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO: 388 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28th March, 2023 \n \n \nNICRA PROJECT \n \n*388.  SHRI VELUSAMY P.:  \nDR. VISHNU PRASAD M.K.:  \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE be pleased to \nstate:- \n \n \n(a) the salient features of National Innovations in Climate Resilient Agriculture (NICRA) \nproject; \n(b) the achievements of this flagship network project in the last three years;  \n(c) the budget allocated and utilised under the NICRA project during the last three \nyears and the current year, year-wise;  \n(d) whether it is a fact that there was under utilization of funds during the said period; \nand  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor along with the steps taken for \noptimum utilisation of the allocated funds? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृ षि और षकसान कल्याण मंत्री      (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\n2 \n \nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 388 TO BE ANSWERED ON 28TH MARCH, 2023 REGARDING \n“NICRA PROJECT” \n \n(a)  Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR), Ministry of Agriculture and \nFarmers Welfare, Government of India launched a flagship network project namely \nNational Innovations in Climate Resilient Agriculture (NICRA) in 2011. The project \naims to develop and promote climate resilient agriculture to address vulnerable areas \nof the countr y and help the districts and regions to cope up with extreme weather \nconditions like droughts, floods, frost, heat waves, etc. The project has three \ncomponents viz. strategic research, technology demonstration and capacity building. \nThe main thrust areas c overed are (i) identification of the most vulnerable \ndistricts/regions, (ii) development of crop varieties and management practices for \nadaptation & mitigation and (iii) assessment of climate change impacts on livestock, \nfisheries and poultry vis-a-vis identification of adaptation strategies. \n \n(b):  Major Achievements of this flagship project during the last three years are: \ni. Assessed risk and vulnerability of 573 districts out of 650 predominantly \nagriculture districts of India as per Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change \n(IPCC) protocol. A total of 109 districts are categorized as very high and 201 \ndistricts as highly vulnerable. \nii. Updated District Agriculture Contingency Plans for 356 districts. \niii. Estimated carbon accumulation and storage of 5 wetlands in West Bengal. \niv. Prepared risk map of classical swine fever for Assam state, African swine fever \nfor north-eastern hill region and avian influenza for India. \nv. Location-specific climate resilient technologies have been tested and validated \nat on -farm sites o f 151 climatically vulnerable districts for adoption by the \nfarmers. \nvi. Identified 366 promising climate resilient technologies for 6 states \n(Maharashtra, Karnataka, Assam, Himachal Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh and \nOdisha) and shared with respective state governments for further upscaling. \nvii. Conducted 3858 capacity building programs benefiting 94394 stakeholders \nduring the last 3 years. \n \n(c) to (e): The details of budget allocated and utilized under NICRA project during \nthe last three years and current year (Rs. In crores) is as follows: \nYear Sanctioned Utilized  % \nUtilization  \n2019-20 46.00 40.58 88 \n2020-21 49.83 44.74 90 \n2021-22 47.00 46.06 98 \n2022-23 40.87 32.64* 80 \n*Funds utilized up to 31st December 2022 \nThe expenditure during the year 2019 -20 and 2020-21 was relatively less due \nto COVID pandemic situation. For last two years, optimum utilization of allotted funds \nwas ensured by close and regular monitoring of the financial milestones with a clear \nroadmap.   \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_389": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 389 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2023 \n \nMORTALITY DUE TO SNAKE BITE \n \n389. SHRIMATI SHARDABEN ANILBHAI PATEL: \n SHRI MITESH RAMESHBHAI PATEL (BAKABHAI): \n  \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the World Health Organization has reported that about 58,000 people are \ndying due to snake bite in the country and, if so, the steps taken/ being ta ken by the \nGovernment to reduce such mortality rate; \n(b) whether it is a fact that about 90 percent deaths occured due to snake bite in rural areas \nand, if so, the steps taken/being taken by the Government to set up medical \ninfrastructure for the same in rural areas; \n(c) whether the existing manufacturing units are capable for producing anti -venom \nmedicines in enough quantity to treat the patients of snake bite; and \n(d) if so, the production capacities-wise details of the companies thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND \nFAMILY WELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n \n(a) to (d):  \nAs per the information received from WHO country office, India, WHO has not released \nany report on mortality due to snakebites. \nAs per information received from St ates/UTs, 626 deaths have been reported in year \n2020.  \nGovernment of India provides financial assistance to all states under National Health \nMission (NHM) for strengthening public health care system including setting up of new \nhospitals and infrastructure upgradation under various schemes including management of \n\nsnake bite cases. Further to ensure drugs availability, Anti -Snake Venom (ASV), has \nalready been included in the National Essential Drug List and State Governments have \nalso been communicated to include ASV in essential drug list. \nAs per approval in Mission Steering Group (MSG) of NHM, additional activities have \nbeen added for prevention and control of snake bites for implementation across all \nstates/UTs from the financial year 2023 -24. These include  awareness on snake bite \nprevention and first aid, capacity building of frontline health workers and IEC activities. \nThe detail of the Manufacturers for Anti -Snake Venom Serum with installed capacity is \nas follows:  \nS.  \nNo. \nName of Manufacturer Installed Capacity \n(Vials/annum)  \n1. M/s Virchow Biotech Pvt. Ltd.  4,32,000  \n2. M/s Haffkine Biopharmaceuticals Corporation Limited  4,50,000  \n3. M/s Biological E. Limited 8,40,000  \n4. M/s Premium Serums and Vaccines Pvt. Ltd. 14,40,000  \n5. M/s Bharat Serums and Vaccines Limited 21,00,000 \n6. M/s VINS Bioproducts Limited 13,60,000  \n7. M/s Central Research Institute 10,000  \n8. M/s Serum Institute of India Pvt. Ltd., Pune 1,20,000  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_390": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 390 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nRATES FIXED UNDER AYUSHMAN BHARAT YOJANA \n \n390. SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware about the problems being faced by the hospitals for getting \nlow rates for treatment under the Ayushman Bharat Yojana  as compared to the market cost for a \ntreatment and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to increase the current rates of treatment and if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government also proposes to increase the rates of the treatment for medical \nmanagement and heart patients and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the measures proposed to be taken by the Government to resolve the problem of getting \nlower rates by the private hospitals under the Ayushman Bharat Yojana? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND \nFAMILY WELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) to (d): National Health Authority (NHA) –the implementing agency of Ayushman Bharat –\nPradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB -PMJAY) has launched revised Health Benefit \nPackages (HBPs) on 06 th April, 2022. HBP 2022 consists of 1,949 treatment procedures \nincluding secondary, tertiary and palliative care across 27 specialties. Rates of treatment \nprocedures under HBP have been fixed as per evidence on cost, stakeholders’ consultation and \nreview of scheme utilization. Further, a study on Costing of Health Servi ces in India (CHSI) \nalso served to take informed decision in this regard. Implementing States/UTs have been \nprovided the flexibility to add State specific packages over and above the national dataset. Also, \nthey have been provided the flexibility to change  the cost of the package as per local \nrequirements. \nUnder HBP 2022, a concept of differential pricing has been introduced to account for regional \nvariations in the cost of service delivery. High end drugs, consumables and diagnostics have \nbeen unbundled fr om the per bed per day cost of medical packages. Overall, rates for 832 \nprocedures have been increased under HBP 2022. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_391": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 391 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03rd February, 2023 \n  \nIncrease in Prices of Drugs \n \n391.  SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.:  \nSHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the prices of Price Controlled Scheduled Bulk Drugs have been hiked by 10.7 \npercent with the approval of National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority, if so, the details \nthereof along with the name of the drugs whose price has been hiked;  \n(b) whether the hike in prices of scheduled medicines is also applicable for the drugs available \nat the Jan Aushadhi Kendras established under PMBJP; and  \n(c) the details of the measures taken by the Government to retain/stabilize the price of \nmedicines sold in Jan Aushadhi Kendras under PMBJP after this hike?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n                                     (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA) \n \n(a): The Drugs (Prices Control) Order, 2013 (DPCO, 2013), do not provide for fixing the prices \nof bulk drugs.   However, the ceiling prices of scheduled formulations (branded or generic) \nunder Schedule -I of DPCO, 2013 are fixed by National Pharmaceutical Pricing  Authority \n(NPPA) under the Department of Pharmaceuticals.   Under the provisions of DPCO, 2013 \nannual revision of ceiling prices of scheduled medicines is permissible, based on Wholesale \nPrice Index (WPI) for all commodities in the preceding year.  The annual change in WPI of all \ncommodities with base year 2011 -12 works out as 10.76607% during the calendar year 2021 \nover the corresponding period in 2020. Accordingly, effective from 01.04.2022, the ceiling \nprice of 898 scheduled formulations (of which 889 scheduled formulations fixed under NLEM \n2015) were revised based on WPI. The relevant notification, S.O. No. 1499(E) dated \n30.03.2022 showing the list of medicines is available at the website of NPPA, viz., \nnppaindia.nic.in. This provides for the maximum inc rease permissible, however, increase in \nthe prices of these drugs by the manufacturers is based on commercial consideration and may \nbe lower or same as permissible increase depending on the market dynamics. \n  \n(b) & (c):  Under Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP), dedicated outlets \nknown as Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras  (PMBJKs) have  been opened to \nprovide quality generic medicines at affordable prices. All manufacturers of scheduled \nmedicines, including those sold through Ja n Aushadhi Kendras, have to sell the same within \nthe ceiling price (as revised from time to time, including based on WPI). However, \nPharmaceuticals & Medical Devices Bureau of India (PMBI) ensures that the prices of \nmedicines sold through the Kendras are a t least 50% cheaper than that of branded \nmedicines available in the open market.  \n  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_392": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 392 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY 2023 \n \nTARGETS ACHIEVED UNDER PMSSY \n \n392. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB: \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n(a) Whether the Pradhan Mantri  Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) has been able to \nachieve its objective since its induction and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) Whether adequate  funds have been allocated under th e scheme to implement it \nsuccessfully; and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof alongwith the funds sanctioned, released and utilized under the \nscheme? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY \nWELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a):  The Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) envisages creation of \ntertiary health care capacity in medical education, research and clinical care in the \nunderserved areas of the country. It aims at correcting the regional imbalances in the \navailability of affordable/reliable tertiary healthcare services and augmenting facilities for \nquality medical education in the country. The PMSSY has two components: \n \na. Setting up of All India Institutes of Medical Sciences(AIIMS); and  \nb. Upgrading existing Government  Medical Colleges/Institutions in a phased \nmanner.  \n \nThe details of projects approved under PMSSY are at Annexure-I. \n \n(b) & (c ):  The details of funds allocated and  released under the Pradhan Mantri Swasthya \nSuraksha Yojana (PMSSY) across the country during the last six years, in cluding the current \nyear, is at Annexure-II. \n***** \n \n\nANNEXURE - I \nA. Establishment of 22 new AIIMS under PMSSY \n �\nS.No. State/UT Name of AIIMS \n1. Andhra Pradesh AIIMS, Mangalagiri \n2. Assam AIIMS, Guwahati \n3. Bihar AIIMS, Patna \n4.   AIIMS, Darbhanga \n5. Chhattisgarh AIIMS, Raipur \n6. Gujarat AIIMS, Rajkot \n7. Haryana AIIMS, Rewari \n8. Himachal Pradesh AIIMS, Bilaspur \n9. Jammu & Kashmir AIIMS, Jammu \n10.   AIIMS, Kashmir \n11. Jharkhand AIIMS, Deoghar \n12. Madhya Pradesh AIIMS, Bhopal \n13. Maharashtra AIIMS, Nagpur \n14. Odisha AIIMS, Bhubaneswar \n15. Punjab AIIMS, Bathinda \n16. Rajasthan AIIMS, Jodhpur \n17. Tamil Nadu AIIMS, Madurai \n18. Telangana AIIMS, Bibinagar \n19. Uttar Pradesh AIIMS, Raebareli \n20.   AIIMS, Gorakhpur \n21. Uttarakhand AIIMS, Rishikesh \n22. West Bengal AIIMS, Kalyani \n \nB.  Government Medical Colleges/ Institutes (GMCI) approved for upgradation under \nPMSSY�\n �\nS. \nNo. \nState Name of the GMCIs Approved Infrastructure   \n1 Andhra  Pradesh SVIMS Tirupati Procurement of equipment    \n2 Siddhartha Medical College, \nVijayawada \nSuper Specialty Block   \n3 Govt Medical College, Anantapur Super Specialty Block   \n4 Assam Guwahati Medical College, Guwahati Super Specialty Block   \n5 Assam Medical College, Dibrugarh Super Specialty Block   \n6 Bihar Srikrishna Medical College, \nMuzaffarpur \nSuper Specialty Block   \n7 Govt Medical College, Darbhanga Super Specialty Block   \n8 PMCH Patna Super Specialty Block   \n9 GMC Gaya Super Specialty Block   \n10 GMC Bhagalpur Super Specialty Block   \n11 IGIMS, Patna Regional Institute of \nOphthalmology   \n12 Chhattisgarh GMC Bilaspur Super Specialty Block   \n13 GMC Jagdalpur Super Specialty Block   \n14 Delhi UCMS-GTBH Super Specialty Block/ \nTrauma Centre   \n15 Goa Goa Medical College, Panaji Super Specialty Block   \n16 Gujarat BJ Medical College,  Ahmedabad Procurement of Medical   \n\nEquipment \n17 Govt Medical College, Rajkot Super Specialty Block   \n18 GMC Surat Super Specialty Block   \n19 GMC Bhavnagar Super Specialty Block   \n20 Haryana PGIMS Rohtak Super Specialty Block and \nService Block   \n21 Himachal \nPradesh \nRajendra Prasad Govt. Medical \nCollege, Tanda \nSuper Specialty Block   \n22 Indira Gandhi Medical College, \nShimla \nSuper Specialty Block   \n23 