    "ls|LS17_S11_U_422": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 31ST MARCH, 2023 \n \n \nASSISTANCE FOR KANTI VELUGU PROGRAMME \n \n*422. SHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR:  \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:                   \n \n(a) whether the Union Government is aware that the State Government of Telangana \nhas undertaken Kanti Velugu programme to take up universal eye screening by \ncovering the entire population of the State and conducting eye screening, vision \ntesting and providing medicines for eye ailments and spectacles free of cost;                        \n \n(b) whether the Government is implementing a National Programme for Control of \nBlindness and Visual Impairment throughout the country; and                                                 \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof including the financial and other assistance that the \nMinistry proposes to provide to the Kanti Velugu programme in Telangana? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to ( c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422 FOR 31ST  MARCH, 2023 \n \n(a) to (c)  Kanti Velugu Programme (KVP) is a State Government Programme.  \n \nGovernment of India has launched National Programme for Control of \nBlindness and Visual Impairment (NPCBVI), a centrally sponsored programme in \nthe year 2017.  The programme addresses all causes of blindness throughout the \ncountry uniformly. Apart from cataract, refractive errors,  corneal blindness and \nchildhood blindness, the programme is equally focusing on other eye diseases like \nglaucoma, diabetic retinopathy, ROP, age related macular degeneration etc. \nassociated with life style diseases like diabetes and aging process. \n \nThe fu nds approved in State Programme Implementation Plan (SPIP) \nunder NPCBVI for states are provided by the Ministry. The funds released to the \nstate of Telangana for the last three years under NPCBVI are as under: \n(Rs.in Lakhs) \nYears 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nFund Released 1347.00 1757.64 1125.70 \n \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_423": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN & CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *423 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 31.03.2023 \n  \nSWADHARGREH SCHEME  \n  \n*423. DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \n         SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE: \n             \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Government is implementing SwadharGreh Scheme (SGS);  \n(b) if so, whether the Government has achieved the objectives for which the \nscheme has been launched, if so, the details thereof; along with the challenges \nbeing faced by the Government to overcome the difficulties in the \nimplementation of the scheme; \n(c)  whether the number of SwadharGreh sanctioned so far are sufficient to meet \nthe growing needs of destitute women in the country and if not, the further plan \nof the Government to give a boost to the scheme; \n(d)  whether State Level Monitoring Committee have been set up and meetings \nhave been conducted as per guidelines in all States/UTs, if so, the number of \nmeetings conducted since the launch of the scheme; State-wise; and \n(e) whether there is any complaint/grievance redressal mechanism under SGS and \nif so, the number of complaints received and disposed off since inception of the \nscheme?  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n (a) to (e)   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n  \n\n  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *423 FOR ANSWER ON 31.03.2023 REGARDING \nSWADHARGREH SCHEME ASKED BY DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE AND \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY  TATKARE, HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \n(LS). \n   \n(a) to (c):          The erstwhile SwadharGrehScheme for women in difficult \ncircumstances and Ujjawala Home - for prevention of trafficking; under the newly \napproved Mission Shakti, have been merged and renamed as Shakti Sadan which is \nan lntegrated Relief and Rehabilitation Home for the women in distress situations and \ndifficult circumstances. Shakti Sadan is a demand driven Centrally Sponsored Scheme \nunder which projects are approved on the basis of local need and requirement of the \nStates/UTs.  Total 441 Shakti Sadans (347 SwadharGrehs and 94 Ujjawala Homes) \nare functional in the country. State/UT–wise list of Shakti Sadans is at Annexure-I. \n  \n(d) to (e)          Under the Missions implemented by the Ministry namely, ‘Mission \nShakti’, appropriate monitoring  mechanismsare available at all levels of \nimplementation i.e., at National, State and District levels. The national level Apex \nCommittee headed by Secretary, WCD, the State Level Committee headed by Chief \nSecretary and overall implementation of scheme at District level by Committee headed \nby District Magistrate. The Ministry continuously monitors the progress of the \nimplementation of all schemes through physical and virtual meetings with States/UTs, \nfield visit by the officials of the Ministry and through digital mode and takes appropriate \nmeasures and issues advisories to States/UTs from time to time. \n  \n            Further, Women Helpline under Mission Shakti envisages to provide 24 hours \nemergency and non-emergency response to women, both in public and private spaces \nby linking them with appropriate authority such as Police, One Stop Cenre, Hospital, \nLegal Services etc.   The helpline also supports women in distress with counseling \nservices in addition to providing information about Women Welfare schemes and  \nprogrammes across the country.   The helpline is available for all women and girls \nincluding residents of Swadhar. \n  \n                         \n         \n***** \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n  \nAnnexure with regard to reply to Parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. \n*423 for answer on 31.03.2023 regarding SwadharGreh Scheme asked by \nDr.SubhashRamraoBhamre and Shri Sunil DattatrayTatkare, Hon’ble Members of \nParliament (LS).  \n  \nState/UT-wise number of Shakti Sadans \n  \nS. \nNo \nName of State/UT Shakti Sadans \n(SwadharGreh) (Ujjawala Home) \n1. Andaman & Nicobar Islands 1 0 \n2. Andhra Pradesh 21 5 \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 1 0 \n4. Assam 17 19 \n5. Chandigarh 1 0 \n6. Chhattisgarh 2 2 \n7. Delhi 2 0 \n8. Goa 2 1 \n9. Gujarat 6 0 \n10. Himachal Pradesh 1 0 \n11. Jammu & Kashmir 2 0 \n12. Jharkhand 5 2 \n13. Karnataka 51 14 \n14. Kerala 7 2 \n15. Madhya Pradesh 15 0 \n16. Maharashtra 10 4 \n17. Manipur 23 22 \n18. Meghalaya 2 0 \n19. Mizoram 11 1 \n20. Nagaland 2 0 \n21. Odisha 52 16 \n22. Punjab 2 0 \n23. Rajasthan 8 1 \n24. Sikkim 1 0 \n25. Tamil Nadu 35 0 \n26. Telangana 18 2 \n27. Tripura 3 0 \n28. Uttar Pradesh 13 0 \n29. Uttarakhand 0 2 \n30. West Bengal 33 1 \n  Total 347 94 \n  \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_424": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *424 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 31.03.2023 \n \n“POSHAN ABHIYAAN IN ASSAM” \n  \n*424 SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has been able to achieve the objectives of Poshan \nAbhiyaan in Assam, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(b) the number of Anganwadi Centres in Assam wherein Poshan Abhiyaan is being \nimplemented, district-wise; and  \n(c) whether the Ministry has conducted any study on the results of this scheme in \nAssam, if so, the details and outcome thereof? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n        (a) to (c)   A statement is laid on the table of the House \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *42 4 FOR ANSWER ON 31.03.2023 REGARDING \nPOSHAN ABHIYAAN IN ASSAM ASKED BY SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE, HON’BLE \nMEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT (LS). \n \n(a) Government launched POSHAN Abhiyaan on 8 th March 2018 to address the \nmalaise of malnutrition in the country. The goals of POSHAN Abhiyaan are to achieve \nimprovement in nutritional status of Children from 0 -6 years, Adolescent Girls, \nPregnant Women and Lactating Mothers, in a time bound manner with fixed targets. \nThe Scheme has been launched in all 36 States/UTs of the country. The efforts under \nthe Supplementary Nutrition Programme under Anganwadi Services, Supplementary \nNutrition Programme, revised Scheme for Adolescent Girls and POSHAN Abhiyaan \nhave been rejuvenated and converged as ‘Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0 \n(Mission Poshan 2.0). \n \nFor estimating the number of underweight, malnourished and severely malnourished \nchildren under 5 years of age in the country a nationwide periodic survey is conducted \nby Ministry of Health & Family Welfare which is known as the National Family Health \nSurvey (NFHS). The recently released report of NFHS -5 (2019-21) has reflected a \ndecline in key nutrition indicators as compared to NFHS-4 (2015-16). As per the recent \nreport of NFHS-5 (2019-21), the nutrition indicators for children under 5 years in the \ncountry have improved as compared with NFHS-4 (2015-16). In Assam, Stunting has \ndecreased (from 36.4% to 35.3%), but underweight prevalence, wasting and anemia \nin children under 5 years and in women aged 15-49 years has increased. \n \n61,722 Anganwadi Centres of As sam are registered on the ICT application, Poshan \nTracker. Technology under Poshan Tracker is being leveraged for inter alia, dynamic \nidentification of stunting, wasting, under -weight prevalence among children and last \nmile tracking of nutrition service delivery. The number of beneficiaries registered under \ndifferent categories from the State of Assam, are as given below: \n \nTotal Pregnant \nWomen \nLactating \nMothers \nChildren  \n(0-6 m) \nChildren \n (6m-3y) \nChildren  \n(3-6yrs) \n35,26,039 1,51,139 1,65,177 1,89,667 12,98,955 1721101 \n  \nOut of the 35.26 lakh registered beneficiaries, about 88% are Aadhar-seeded. \n \nFurther, an important focus of the Scheme has been on ‘Behaviour Change \nCommunication’ through Jan Andolan. At the national level, Rashtriya Poshan Maah \nis celebrated in the month of September across the country while Poshan Pakhwada \nis celebrated in March. Over the years, themes have included overall nutrition, \nhygiene, water and sanitation, anemia prevention, importance of breast -feeding, \ngrowth monitor ing, role of Poshan Panchayats, AYUSH for Well -being, Yoga for \nHealth, importance of Poshan Vatikas for cultivation of local vegetables, medicinal \nplants/herbs and fruits at the community level etc. \n \n \n \n \n\nIn Poshan Pakhwada and Poshan Maah 2022, close to 16,000 and 5.9 lakh activities, \nrespectively, were conducted across the State of Assam Malnutrition is a complex and \nmulti-dimensional issue, affected mainly by a number of generic factors including \ninadequate food consumption due to economic and social factors, inequity and gender \nimbalances, poor sanitary and environmental conditions, etc The nutritional status of \nthe population is outcome of complex and inter -related set of factors and cannot be \nimproved by the efforts of single sector alone. Therefore, it needs a multi -pronged \napproach. \n \n(b) POSHAN Abhiyaan is being implemented across all the districts of the state \ncovering 61,722 Anganwadi Centres. The district -wise Anganwadi centres covered \nunder POSHAN Abhiyaan is at Annexure I. \n \n(c) Development Monitoring & Evaluation Office (DMEO) under NITI Aayog has \nconducted Sectoral Evaluation of schemes including POSHAN \nAbhiyaan.  Performance of POSHAN Abhiyaan has been found ‘Satisfactory’ in the \ncategories of Relevance, Sustainability and Equity.  \n\nAnnexure I \nDistrict wise number of Anganwadi Centres in Assam \nDistrict Total Active AWCs \nBaksa 2636 \nBarpeta 2913 \nBiswanath 1839 \nBongaigaon 1116 \nCachar 3941 \nCharaideo 668 \nChirang 920 \nDarrang 2163 \nDhemaji 1557 \nDhubri 2409 \nDibrugarh 2156 \nDima Hasao 613 \nGoalpara 2488 \nGolaghat 2223 \nHailakandi 1374 \nHojai 1923 \nJorhat 1645 \nKamrup 3246 \nKamrup Metro 1021 \nKarbiAnglong 2218 \nKarimganj 1570 \nKokrajhar 1687 \nLakhimpur 3066 \nMajuli 598 \nMorigaon 2132 \nNagaon 3993 \nNalbari 1527 \nSivasagar 1229 \nSonitpur 1989 \nSouth SalmaraMankachar 550 \nTamulpur 0 \nTinsukia 2021 \nUdalguri 1439 \nWest Karbi-Anglong 852 \nTotal 61722 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_425": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH RESEARCH \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 425 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 31ST MARCH, 2023 \n \nBURDEN OF GENETIC DISORDERS \n \n*425. SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has analysed the burden of genetic disorders on health \ncare system in the country and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to address such ‘inherited \ndisorders’ and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has trained medical personnel in Government facilities \nfor prenatal testing for genetic disorders; and  \n \n(d) if so, the number of Government hospitals equipped with such facility for mothers \ncarrying high-risk genetic disorders? \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 425 FOR 31ST  MARCH, 2023 \n \n(a) to (d) The Government has launched National Policy for Rare Diseases (NPRD), \n2021 in March, 2021. The policy aims at lowering the incidence and prevalence of rare \ndiseases based on an integrated and comprehensive preventive strategy encompassing \nawareness generation, screening and counselling programmes to prevent births of \nchildren with rare diseases, and within the constraints on resources and competing \nhealth care priorities, enab le access to affordable health care to Patients of rare \ndiseases. Financial assistance upto Rs. 50 lakhs is provided to the patients suffering \nfrom any category of rare diseases and getting treatment in any of the Centre of \nExcellence (CoEs) identified und er NPRD, 2021. As per NPRD, 2021, eleven (11) \nCoEs have been identified for diagnosis, prevention and treatment of rare diseases. \nIndian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) National Registry of Rare Diseases \nand Other Inherited Disorders (NRROID) initiated in November 2019 documents the \nhospital based data from patients seeking care for rare diseases. The registry at present \ncontains the data of 9163 patients. \nThe Department of Biotechnology (DBT) started the Unique Methods of \nManagement of Inherited Disorde rs (UMMID) Initiative in 2019, which has three \ncomponents. Under the first component, 5 NIDAN (National Inherited Diseases \nAdministration) Kendras (genetic testing laboratories) have been established at \nGovernment hospitals. Under the second component (out reach), prenatal and newborn \nscreening is carried out at District Hospitals at 9 Aspirational Districts, coordinated by \n7 premier research institutions of India.  Under the third component, 6 training centers \nhave been established for training of in -service Government doctors in genetic \ndiagnostics and counseling. Also, PRaGeD mission is the first ever \"Mission on \nPaediatric Rare Genetic Disorders (PraGeD), a countrywide screening programme in \nassociation with 20 other institutions to decode the unknown genetic mutations causing \nsuch diseases. DBT’s autonomous institutions namely, National Institute of \nBiomedical Genomics (NIBMG), Kalyani, West Bengal and Centre for DNA \nFingerprinting & Diagnostics (CDFD), Hyderabad, Telangana provide genetic testing \nservices for Genetic Disorders.   \nSickle cell disease (SCD) is a single gene disorder causing a debilitating \nsystemic syndrome characterized by chronic anaemia, acute painful episodes, organ \ninfarction and chronic organ damage and by a significant reduction in li fe expectancy. \nUnder National Health Mission (NHM), Government of India supports the States for \nprevention and management of sickle cell disease as per their annual PIP proposals. \nThe Ministry has also released technical operational guidelines for preventi on and \ncontrol of hemoglobinopathies in 2016 including sickle cell anaemia.  \n\n \n \n \nIn the Union budget of FY 2023 -24, it has been announced to launch a mission \nto eliminate sickle cell anaemia by 2047. The mission entails focus on awareness \ncreation, universal screening of approximately seven crore people in the 0-40 years age \ngroup in affected tribal areas and counselling through collaborative efforts of central \nministries and state governments. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_426": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n��\n��������� �\n��������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n��\n����������������������������������� �\n��\n����������������������������������� �\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n��\n����� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ���� ����������� ��� ���������������� ������������ ��� �������� ��� ���� ������������\n�����\n����� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������\n���\n��\n������ �\n��\n��������������������������������������� �\n������������������ ������������ �\n��\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������\n��\n���� ��� ���� ����� �������� ����������� �������� ������ ������ ��� �� ���������� ���\n������� ���� ������� ��� �������� �������� ��������� ��� � ����� ������������ ����\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n���� �� ��������� ����������� ���� ��������� ��� ������ ��� ���������������� ������������\n��������� ����� �������� ���� ���������� ��� �������������� ������ ��� ���� ��������� ���\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ���� ������ ������ ������ ����������� ���������� ����� ��� ���� ������� ��� �����\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� �������� ���� ������� ���� ��������� ������������ ��� �� ���������� ������������� ����\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������� �������� ����������� ���� ����� ����������� ��� ���� �������� ��� ������� ��������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ����������� ��� ������ ������ ����������� ���� ������ ��������� ������������� ��� ���������\n�������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n�������������������������\n��\n� �\n����\n����������\n���� �\n�����������\n��������������\n����������������\n�������������\n������������������� ���������� �\n��������������\n������������\n�� ���� ���� ������ ������ ������\n�� ���� ���� ������� ������ ������\n�� ���� ���� ������� ������ ������\n�� ���� ���� ������� ������ ������\n�� ���� ���� ������ ���������� ������\n��\n���������������������������������������������\n��\n�����\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_427": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 427 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3rd  FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nCGHS FACILITIES TO AUTONOMOUS INSTITUTIONS \n \n427.  SHRI TIRATH SINGH RAWAT:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is not providing facilities of CGHS plastic card and medicine to \nthe employees of Central Government autonomous bodies such as Central Sanskrit University, \nShri Lal Bahadur Shastri National Sanskrit University and National Sanskrit Universi ty, \nTirupati; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is not providing CGHS plastic card and medicine facility to these \ncentral autonomous bodies due to financial constraints; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND \nFAMILY WELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) to (d) Central Government Health Scheme (CGHS) is a contributory health scheme primarily \nmeant for central government employees and pensioners. The scheme provides OPD \nfacilities and medicines to the beneficiaries through CGHS Wellness Centres. The \nAutonomous Bodies/ Statutory Bodies have been issued plastic card s. However, there is also \nan option of printing a paper card with a beneficiary under CGHS. The Autonomous \nBodies/Statutory Bodies registered with CGHS are entitled for availing OPD and Medicine \nfacilities through CGHS Wellness Centres. Further, Autonomous Bodies/ Statutory Bodies \nwhich are not covered under CGHS scheme, have the option to adopt Central Service s \n(Medical Attendance) Rules, 1944 for availing medical facilities or any other medical \nscheme as chosen by the Autonomous Body/ Statutory Body. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_428": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 428 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 31ST MARCH, 2023 \n \nDATA SECURITY AND PRIVACY UNDER AB-PMJAY \n \n*428. SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is collecting personal data of the beneficiaries under \nAyushman Bharat - Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana and if so, the details thereof \nalong with the kind of data which is being collected;  \n \n(b) whether the Government is collecting the biometric data for the Yojana and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether it is mandatory to collect the biometric data to extend the benefits of the \nYojana to the beneficiari es and if so, the details thereof along with the biometric \ninformation collected by the Government and private players in furthering various \ninitiatives under the Yojana, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(d) whether the data such as biometrics, personal health record, personal information \nhas been collected after obtaining the consent of the citizens under the Yojana, and if \nso, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(e) the details of  repository in which the data collected under this scheme is being \nstored along with the steps that have been taken to keep the data secure? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to ( e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 428 FOR 31ST  MARCH, 2023 \n \n(a) to (e) Ayushman Bharat - Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB -PMJAY) \nlaunched on 23.09.2018 is the largest publicly funded health protection scheme in the \nworld which provides health cover of Rs. 5 lakh per family per year for secondary \nand tertiary care hospitalization to 60 crore beneficiaries through a network of 26,892 \nhospitals empanelled in the country. AB -PMJAY is a notified s cheme under Section \n7 of Aadhaar Act. During e-KYC/authentication, personal details like name, guardian \nname, gender and address of beneficiaries are collected. Further, at the time of \ntreatment, beneficiary's medical details are collected. The consent of the beneficiary \nis duly recorded every time the data are collected. Data collected under the scheme \nare secure and stored in data warehouse in an encrypted form in accordance with \nestablished Application Data Security Norms. National Health Authority (NHA)  has \non boarded Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology (MeitY) empanelled \nCloud Service Provider with its Data Centers in India. As per MeitY policy, \nnecessary actions are taken to safeguard against all types of security breaches \nincluding Data  Security. NHA has also engaged Telecom Consultants of India \nLimited (TCIL) as Security Operation Center (SOC) who monitors security postures \n(alerts & incidents) of the cloud infrastructure. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_429": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 429 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03/02/2023 \n \nWORLD AYURVEDA FOUNDATION \n \n \n429. SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI: \n \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Arogya Fairs are helping to spread, promote and propagate AYUSH in the \ncountry including Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whetherany collaborative efforts have been made by the Government in co -operation \nwith the World Ayurveda Foundation to promote Indian Ayurveda in other countries; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof;  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, the National  and State Arogya Fairs organised under the Central Sector \nScheme for Promotion of Information, Education and Communication (IEC) in Ayush is \nhelping to spread, promote and propagate Ayush in the country.  The Ministry has organised 2 \nState level Arogya Fair s during the Financial Year  2021-22 and has organised 2  National \nArogya Fairs & 1 State Arogya Fair in the Current Financial Year 2022-23. \n(c) & (d): The Ministry of Ayush and its Autonomous Bodies have collaborated with World \nAyurveda Foundation to promote Ayurveda at National and International Level. Ministry of \nAyush has recently supported the World Ayurveda Foundation in the organization of the 9th \nWorld Ayurveda Congress & AROGYA Expo in Panjim, Goa  during 8 th to 11 th December \n2022.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_430": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED Q.NO. 430\nANSWERED ON 31.03.2023\n PASSPORT SERVICE CENTRES\n*430. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA\n          SHRI KISHAN KAPOOR\nWill the Minister of External Affairs be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of Passport Service Centres opened in Rajasthan and Himachal Pradesh during the last\nthree years and the current year;\n(b) the details of the funds sanctioned/allocated and utilized thereon during the said period;\n(c) the number of applications for passports pending in Rajasthan at present;\n(d) the action taken by the Government to clear the pending passports in time; and\n(e) whether the Government has any proposal for setting up of new passport office in the said States\nincluding Champa district and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER FOR EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n (DR. SUBRAHMANYAM JAISHANKAR)\n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n*****\n\nSTATEMENT  REFERRED  TO  IN  REPLY  TO  PARTS  (a)  TO  (e)  OF  THE  LOK  SABHA\nSTARRED  QUESTION  NO.  *430  REGARDING  “ PASSPORT  SERVICE  CENTRES ”  FOR\nANSWER ON 31.03.2023.\n(a) to (e): The Ministry in association with Department of Posts (DOP) had announced in January, 2017\nopening of Post Office Passport Seva Kendras (POPSKs) at the Head Post Offices (HPO)/Post Offices\n(PO) in each Parliamentary constituency where there is no Passport Seva Kendra (PSK) or Post Office\nPassport Seva Kendra (POPSK). POPSKs are established in remote locations and small towns in the Lok\nSabha constituencies, thereby bringing passports services more closer to the citizens and making it easier\nfor citizens to obtain Passports. All the Lok Sabha Constituencies in Rajasthan & Himachal Pradesh have\na PSK or a POPSK. The expenditure on opening of PSK and POPSKs is met out of the sanctioned budget\nof Ministry of External Affairs directly without any earmarking of funds for particular States.\nDue to rise in demand and backlog resulted from the Covid-19 pandemic, the Ministry has been working\ncontinuously to speed up Passport issuance and meet high or seasonal demand for Passport by taking\nvarious steps including increasing the number of daily appointments and organising Special Drives or\nMelas on weekends. The Ministry conducted  special drives in September-October 2022 to reduce the\ndemands for Police Clearance Certificate and Passport Melas on 3 rd, 10th, 17th and 24 th December 2022,\n25th February, 4th and 18th March 2023 in various Passport offices. \nSince the end of lockdown (01.6.2021 to 28.02.2023), 5,95,940 passports have been issued in Rajasthan\nand a total of 5527 number of applications are at various stages of processing. \n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_431": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 431 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2023 \n  \nPROJECTS UNDER NIRBHAYA FUND  \n  \n431.   SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)   the details of the projects approved under Nirbhaya Fund, State-wise; \n(b)   the details of the proposals received from various authorities for utilization of \nNirbhaya Fund from the State of Tamil Nadu; \n(c)   the details of fund allocated to the State of Tamil Nadu during the last five years; \n(d)   whether any audit or monitoring of Nirbhaya Fund utilization has been done so far; \nand  \n(e)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n        (SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n  \n(a) to (c) : Presently  , a total of 38 projects  with total project cost of Rs. 9228.50 \ncrore  stand  appraised by the Empowered Committee (EC) for funding under Nirbhaya \nFund. The projects/ schemes appraised by Empowered Committee (EC) under \nFramework for Nirbhaya Fund are having staggered implementation schedule. While \nsome of the appraised projects ar e directly implemented by Central Ministries/ \nDepartments; majority of the projects are implemented through State Governments/ Union \nTerritory Administrations in which Central Government releases the funds to States/ UTs \nas per prescribed fund -sharing pattern of respective projects/ schemes. The details of \nprojects approved under Nirbhaya Fund are at Annexure-I. \n  \nThe proposal received from various authorities for implementation in Tamil Nadu \nunder Nirbhaya Fund includes proposals for Emergency Response Support System, One \nStop Centre, Universalisation of Women Helpline, Central Victim compensation fund, \nCyber Crime Prevention against Women & Children (CCPWC), Setting up and \nstrengthening Anti Human Trafficking Units, Setting up and strengthening of Women Help \nDesks in Police Stations, Setting up of Fast Track Special courts, Proposal   for \ncustomization, deployment and management of State-wise vehicle tracking platform, Safe \nCity Project, Strengthening of DNA analysis, Cyber Forensic and related facilities in State \nForensic Laboratories, etc. As reported by concerned Ministries / Departments / \nImplementing Agencies, a total of Rs. 314.28 crore has been allocated / released to State \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu  during the year 2017-18 to 2021-22.   \n  \n(d) & (e): An Empowered Committee (EC) of Officers constituted under the Framework for \nNirbhaya Fund  appraises and recommends the proposals for funding under Nirbhaya \nFund and reviews the status of implementation of approved projects from time to time in \nconjunction with the concerned Ministries/ Departments/ Implementing Agencies (IAs). \nThe Ministries/ Departments/ IAs also review and monitor the progress of their respective \nSchemes/ projects to ensure faster implementation at their level. \n***** \n\n  \n \n  \n Annexure-I \n  \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a)  to (c) of Lok Sabha Un-starred Question No. \n431 for answer on 03.02.2023  regarding ‘Projects under Nirbhaya Fund’  \n  \nDetails of the projects appraised under Nirbhaya Fund since its inception \n  \n (Rs. in crore) \n  \nMinistries/ \nDepartments \nSl. No. Project Name Date of appraisal by \nEC \nFund \nappraised \nby EC \nMinistry of \nHome Affairs \n  \n1.   Emergency   Response \nSupport system (ERSS) \n02.02.2014   (CCEA) 321.69 \n2.   Creation of Central Victim \nCompensation Fund \n(CVCF) \nJune, 2015  200.00 \n3.   Cyber Crime Prevention \nagainst Women & \nChildren (CCPWC) \n06.01.2016 195.83 \nSub-project   under \nCCPWC \n04.06.2018 28.93 \n4.   Proposal for providing \nfacility of Social Workers/ \nCounsellors at \nthe   District and Sub - \nDivisional Police Station \nLevel in Delhi \n18.02.2016 5.0